{"id":"rdb-ef5811","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-c3129033-bb76-4797-8d0e-864710c3d183","text":"Implementation complete. Changes:\n\n**catalog.go:**\n- Added `EnumValues []string` field to `columnDisk` (backward-compatible via `omitempty`)\n- Extended `decodeType()` signature with `enumValues []string`, added `Type_ENUM` case using `types.CreateEnumType()`\n- Updated `decodeSchema()` and `validateSchema()` to pass enum values through\n- Updated `encodeSchema()` to extract values from `sql.EnumType`\n- Added `rawValue()` case for ENUM (uint16 serialization)\n- Added `decodeRow()` case for ENUM (uint16 deserialization)\n\n**engine.go:**\n- Added enum uint16→string conversion in `Query()` results using `EnumType.At()`, so the API returns string values (MySQL-compatible) while the engine operates on uint16 indices internally\n\n**Decision not in spec:** The go-mysql-server engine returns enum values as `uint16` indices in row iterators. I added a conversion step in `Session.Query()` that resolves indices to strings via `EnumType.At()` before returning results. This keeps the storage layer and engine internals using uint16 (correct for ORDER BY, comparisons) and only converts at the API boundary.\n\n**Tests added:** DDL, insert/select round-trip, invalid value rejection, nullable, default values, ORDER BY (definition order), persistence through reopen. All pass. Full test suite green."},"created_at":"2026-09-14T19:31:42.342095Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.270 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-ef5811","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-09-14T19:42:58.054876Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.270 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-ef5811","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-35cece4d-2047-440c-97ee-828f46c5ad3b","text":"Fixed both review findings:\n\n**P1 — ALTER TABLE MODIFY COLUMN enum index resolution:** Before converting row values to the new type, the old enum uint16 index is now resolved to its string value via `EnumType.At()`. This ensures that reordering enum values (e.g., `ENUM('red','green')` → `ENUM('green','red')`) preserves the string identity of stored data. Added `TestEnumAlterReorderPreservesValues` test.\n\n**P2 — Non-default collation rejection:** `validateSchema()` now rejects ENUM columns with non-default collation, consistent with RepoDB's approach for all types (strings also hardcode `sql.Collation_Default`). This prevents the silent collation-mismatch-after-reopen bug.\n\nSpec updated to reflect both changes. Full test suite passes."},"created_at":"2026-09-14T19:44:35.81483Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.270 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
