{"id":"rdb-207d5c","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Windows: read-only (0444) object files break concurrent writes and deletion","description":"`Filesystem.Put` in `common/storage/filesystem.go` writes each object to a temp file, calls `tmp.Chmod(0o444)`, then `os.Rename`s it into place. On Windows, Go maps 0444 to `FILE_ATTRIBUTE_READONLY`, and that attribute blocks:\n\n- **Rename onto the path.** `Put` checks with `os.Stat` and then renames, so two writers creating the same content-addressed object can both pass the stat. The second rename targets a read-only file and fails with \"Access is denied\". On Unix that rename just replaces an identical object.\n- **Deletion.** `os.Remove` on a read-only file fails, so anything that deletes objects (cache cleanup, gc, tests' temp dirs) breaks. The deferred `os.Remove(tmpName)` in `Put` also can't clean up the 0444 temp file when the rename fails, so the temp file leaks.\n\n## Fix\nDrop the `Chmod(0o444)` or make it Unix-only (content addressing already protects integrity). Otherwise, clear the read-only attribute before any rename-over or remove. Either way, when the rename fails because the destination already exists, count it as success since the content is identical.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Concurrent `Put` of the same object from two writers succeeds on Windows\n- [ ] Objects and leftover temp files can be removed on Windows\n- [ ] Unix behavior unchanged; `go test ./...` passes\n\nFound while scoping Windows support (rdb-52da05, whose spec leaves runtime Windows issues out of scope).","sort_order":"a8","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-207d5c","target_id":"rdb-52da05","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:05:15.511057Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-6e40a9","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Windows: os.Rename fails when target is open in another process (no FILE_SHARE_DELETE)","description":"Go on Windows opens files without `FILE_SHARE_DELETE`. While any process holds a file open for reading, an `os.Rename` onto that path (or an `os.Remove` of it) fails with a sharing violation. On Unix the rename succeeds atomically, and readers keep reading the old inode.\n\nAffected atomic-replace sites:\n- `integration/conflicts.go` (`writeConflicts` → `os.Rename(name, path)` onto `conflicts/\u003cremote\u003e.json`; `os.Remove(conflictPath(...))` has the same problem)\n- `common/storage/filesystem.go` (`Put` → `os.Rename(tmpName, path)` onto an object file that a concurrent reader may have open)\n\n## Fix\nWrap rename (and remove) at these sites in a small helper. On Windows it retries with short backoff on `ERROR_ACCESS_DENIED` / `ERROR_SHARING_VIOLATION` for a bounded time, which is the same approach the Go toolchain's `robustio` uses. On Unix it's a plain `os.Rename`. After the retries run out, return the error as today.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] Rename onto `conflicts/\u003cremote\u003e.json` and onto object files succeeds on Windows when a reader briefly holds the target open\n- [ ] Retry is bounded and applies only on Windows\n- [ ] `go test ./...` passes; Windows cross-compile clean\n\nRelated: the 0444 read-only object issue has the same call site in `Put`.","sort_order":"a9","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-6e40a9","target_id":"rdb-52da05","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:05:15.532083Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-207d5c","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-207d5c","target_id":"rdb-52da05","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-207d5c","target_id":"rdb-6e40a9","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:05:19.178437Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-52da05","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-a7827c73-d725-4dbd-9091-75cb52e6e8e4","text":"Implementation ready for review.\n\n**Changes**\n- `common/repository/repository.go`: added a shared `acquireFileLock(ctx, path)` built on `gofrs/flock`. `Repository.lock` (publish.lock) calls it.\n- `common/repository/working.go`: `WorkingState.lock` (working.lock) calls the same helper. The `w.mu` ordering is unchanged, and `w.mu` is still released on every error path.\n- `repository_test.go`: the lock-cancellation test holds the publish lock with `flock.TryLock`.\n- `peakrss_unix[_test].go` / `peakrss_other[_test].go` in `integration` and `experiments/dbbench`: max-RSS is behind `//go:build unix` / `!unix`. Non-unix platforms report it as unavailable.\n- go.mod: `github.com/gofrs/flock v0.13.1` (direct).\n\n**Decision not in spec**\n- `flock.TryLockContext` fails immediately if ctx is already done, even when the lock is free. The old loop tried the lock once before checking ctx. The helper keeps the old behavior by calling `TryLock()` once before falling back to `TryLockContext`.\n\n**Verification**\n- darwin: `go vet ./...` and `go test ./...` pass. The lock tests also pass with `-race`.\n- `GOOS=windows` amd64 build + vet pass, arm64 build passes, and `GOOS=linux` vet passes.\n- The sync benchmark still reports `peak-rss-bytes` on darwin.\n- No `unix.Flock` usages remain.\n\nNot validated at runtime on a real Windows host (out of scope per spec)."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:07:50.752879Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-ef8c25","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Add GOOS=windows cross-compile build + vet to make test","description":"Once rdb-52da05 makes RepoDB compile for Windows, keep it compiling. Development happens on macOS, so a new Unix-only call (`x/sys/unix`, syscall constants, etc.) would go unnoticed until a Windows user hit it.\n\nAdd a Windows cross-compile check to the Makefile and run it as part of `make test`:\n\n```make\nGOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go build ./...\nGOOS=windows GOARCH=amd64 go vet ./...\n```\n\n`go vet` also type-checks `_test.go` files, so test code stays portable too.\n\n## Acceptance criteria\n- [ ] `make test` runs the Windows build + vet (a separate target is fine, as long as `make test` calls it)\n- [ ] `make test` fails if either Windows step fails\n- [ ] Passes on the current tree once rdb-52da05 is merged\n\n## Out of scope\nRunning the test suite on a real Windows host (e.g. a GitHub Actions `windows-latest` job). Cross-compiling won't catch runtime bugs like rdb-207d5c / rdb-6e40a9. That's deliberately deferred.","sort_order":"aA","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-ef8c25","target_id":"rdb-52da05","kind":"depends_on"},{"source_id":"rdb-ef8c25","target_id":"rdb-207d5c","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-ef8c25","target_id":"rdb-6e40a9","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["task"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:09:25.851688Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-52da05","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:10:56.460295Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-52da05","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-69531f2b-3950-4085-8096-647fdcaf94f9","text":"Revision for review finding [P3] \"Unavailable RSS is reported as zero\" (spec updated first).\n\n- `experiments/dbbench/main.go`: `report.PeakRSS` is now `*int64` with `json:\"process_peak_rss_bytes,omitempty\"`. It is set only when `peakRSS()` succeeds. When unavailable, the JSON field is omitted and the summary prints `Process peak RSS: unavailable`. Existing scorecard JSON keeps the same shape when RSS is measured.\n- `main_test.go`: `TestReportOmitsUnavailablePeakRSS` checks that the field is omitted when nil and serialized when set.\n- The integration benchmark needed no change: it already skips `ReportMetric` when RSS is unavailable.\n\nVerified: dbbench tests pass. `go vet ./...` passes on darwin, windows/amd64 and linux, and windows/arm64 builds."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:11:31.582133Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
