{"id":"rdb-207d5c","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-a220bf38-1f82-4132-ade6-ef2c6be3156c","text":"Implementation ready for review.\n\n**Changes** (`common/storage/filesystem.go`)\n- `Chmod(0o444)` now runs only when `runtime.GOOS != \"windows\"`. Windows objects and temp files are writable and removable, and Unix objects stay 0444.\n- When the rename fails and the destination is already a regular file, `Put` returns `hash, nil`: another writer won the race with identical content. Any other failure returns the original rename error. A directory or other non-regular file at the object path still fails.\n- A package-level `var rename = robustio.Rename` lets tests simulate a lost race.\n\n**Tests** (new `common/storage/filesystem_test.go`; the package had none)\n- Round trip.\n- 16 concurrent `Put`s of the same object all succeed, with no leftover temp files.\n- Lost race: a stubbed rename creates the destination and then fails, and `Put` still succeeds.\n- Real rename failure is returned, and the temp file is cleaned up.\n- The object mode is 0444 (skipped on Windows).\n- Mutation check: turning off the lost-race check makes `TestFilesystemPutLostRaceSucceeds` fail.\n\n**Verification:** `make test` passes, including the Windows build + vet. The storage tests also pass with `-race`.\n\n**Not verified:** behavior on a real Windows host. This fix also removes the 2s retry delay noted in rdb-6e40a9, since the rename destination is no longer read-only on Windows."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T07:24:43.165536Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
