{"id":"rdb-6ae82d","type":"MERGE","payload":{"branch":"rdb-6ae82d-refresh-docs-benchmarks-latest-md-with-a-full-run","base_sha":"57a0cd8","merge_sha":"","strategy":"squash"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:04:47.248724Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-6ae82d","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DONE","sort_order":"aX","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:04:47.275703Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-92b9e2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"rdb-92b9e2","target_id":"rdb-59c7d7"},{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"rdb-92b9e2","target_id":"rdb-515fae"}],"description":"Found analysing the rdb-6ae82d scorecard. Journal mode spent 2,942 of 2,970 s in sync workloads, and journal sync latency grows ~4× over 30 consecutive syncs (divergent merge at 1k rows 3.6 s → 14.8 s), while native-git stays flat.\n\n**Root cause** (git-call probe, journal, 1k rows, 10 requests): per sync, the number of distinct commits passed to `ls-tree -r` grows by one per sync per clone (2, 4, … 22), which is the entire data history, loaded several times per sync. On a cache miss, `WorkingState.load` (`common/repository/working.go`) replays the whole journal from offset 0. It calls `repo.SnapshotCommit` (ls-tree + manifest read + validation) for **every** `checkpoint` record, and for every `commit` whose base changed, and discards all but the last. The journal is never truncated (rdb-515fae), so replay cost is O(history) and a run of syncs is quadratic.\n\n**Fix:** make replay track the base as a commit ID and load the snapshot lazily: only when a later prepare must be applied on top of it, or once at the end. Replay should then load O(1) snapshots regardless of history length.\n\nLikely explains most of rdb-59c7d7.","estimate":2,"labels":["bug","performance"],"sort_order":"a0","status":"PLANNED","title":"Journal replay loads a snapshot for every historical checkpoint"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:05:16.437313Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-586f81","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"From the rdb-6ae82d scorecard: range (100 rows), ordered LIMIT, full scan and join all cost 87–96 ms at 50k rows in both RepoDB modes (17–19 ms at 10k), against 0.3–36 ms for MySQL and Dolt. Every such query decodes the whole table (`ensureRows` / `partitionRows` loads all entries), so `ORDER BY id LIMIT 20` and `WHERE id BETWEEN …` pay for a full scan.\n\nDirection: implement range lookups on the primary index (`LookupPartitions` currently supports point lookups only) using `prolly` iterators from the lower bound; stream rows instead of materializing `s.rows`; and let GMS push down LIMIT/order where the index order matches. Merge the pending overlay during iteration. Expected: range/limit queries become proportional to rows returned, not table size.","estimate":5,"labels":["feature","performance"],"sort_order":"aB","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Push LIMIT and key ranges down into the Prolly tree iterator"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:05:16.466953Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2c93ca","type":"CREATE","payload":{"description":"From the rdb-6ae82d analysis: even the first journal sync starts ~47 git subprocesses, and native-git sync costs a flat 1.3–3.3 s per divergent merge or conflict round trip. Only ~55% of sync wall time is spent inside git; the rest is in-process work around it.\n\nDirection:\n- Profile one sync end to end (git calls plus Go pprof).\n- Keep a long-lived `cat-file --batch` per repository instead of per snapshot.\n- Avoid re-listing and re-validating snapshots whose commit was already validated in this process.\n- Batch `rev-parse`/`ls-remote` calls.\n\nMeasure first; do this after the journal replay fix, since that removes most of the per-sync growth.","estimate":5,"labels":["task","performance"],"sort_order":"aC","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Reduce git subprocesses per sync (reuse cat-file --batch, skip redundant snapshot reloads)"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T13:05:16.487859Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
