{"id":"rdb-ee17d5","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-3ee1e245-cab9-4559-b5df-6ae044df861b","text":"Implementation ready for review (branch `rdb-ee17d5-faster-row-encoding-replace-json-row-blobs-during`).\n\n**Changes**\n- `engine/rowcodec.go` (new): binary row codec as specced. Column count, NULL bitmap, then cells: varints for ints, ENUM and TIME; fixed 8-byte LE for datetimes; raw IEEE bits for floats; length-prefixed strings, binary and JSON; DECIMAL as the unscaled integer at the column scale (a small int64 form and a big form). Decoding builds column-typed Go values directly, with no `Convert` except for JSON. Decoding is strict: truncated rows, trailing bytes and width mismatches are errors.\n- `engine/catalog.go`: the JSON codec (`cellDisk`, old `encodeRow`/`decodeRow`, `rawValue`) is removed. Callers are unchanged.\n- `FormatVersion` 2 → 3 and `workingFormatVersion` 2 → 3. The format test is version-independent and now asserts that a format-(N-1) repository is refused.\n- Docs: `storage-format.md` has a row encoding (format 3) section; `fuzz-testing.md` lists `FuzzDecodeRow`.\n\n**Tests**\n- `engine/rowcodec_test.go`: round-trips for every supported type at its extremes, checking:\n  - decoded values equal the `Convert` output;\n  - decoded Go types match;\n  - re-encoding gives identical bytes.\n- Also a wide row (bitmap spanning 3 bytes), canonical-bytes checks (1.5 vs 1.50, −0 decimal, the same instant in different time zones), a size check, every truncation prefix rejected, and `FuzzDecodeRow`.\n- `TestAlterTableModifyDecimalScaleRoundTrips`: changes a DECIMAL column's scale, reopens the engine, and checks the values.\n- `make test`, `go test -race ./...` and `make lint` pass. `FuzzDecodeRow` and the 5 existing row fuzz targets ran 20 s each without failures.\n\n**Decisions not in the spec**\n- Empty BLOB values now decode as a non-nil `[]byte{}`. The old codec returned `[]byte(nil)`.\n- String cells share one `string(data)` copy per row.\n\n**Benchmarks** (M1, 50k rows, journal, median of 3, before → after)\n| case | time | allocs/op |\n|---|---|---|\n| fullscan | 66.4 → 31.8 ms (−52%) | 857.5k → 507.9k |\n| range10k | 13.3 → 6.34 ms (−52%) | 172.5k → 102.5k |\n| range100 | 0.242 → 0.137 ms | 2507 → 1708 |\n| limit20 | 0.130 → 0.073 ms | 1124 → 832 |\n| after-limit20 | 0.102 → 0.083 ms | 1192 → 1058 |\n| RowCodec decode (8-col mixed row) | 2149 → 197 ns | 32 → 9 |\n| RowCodec encode | 1616 → 84 ns | 30 → 1 |\n\nThe mixed benchmark row shrinks from ~200 to 55 bytes."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:50:29.751168Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-4c3ae3","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Position RepoDB as a relational database that users can clone, query, branch, modify, inspect, merge, and synchronize through Git—whether or not the repository contains application code.\n\nThis roadmap begins after the current Tier 1 implementation and performance work establishes a credible supported workload envelope. It deliberately separates table-stakes adoption work from the differentiated version-control experience.\n\n## Product outcome\n\nRepoDB supports two complementary deployment modes:\n\n1. Embedded/local-first applications with explicit Git synchronization.\n2. A securely operated standalone MySQL-compatible server backed by the same cloneable, versioned data history.\n\n## Roadmap outcomes\n\n- Historical snapshots, diffs, named data branches, switching, merging, restore, and revert are first-class operations.\n- Standalone network service has secure defaults, authentication, TLS, authorization, and operational controls.\n- Common applications can rely on generated keys, referential integrity, and a tested compatibility profile.\n- Backup, recovery, compaction, format migration, and history maintenance make long-lived repositories operable.\n\n## Sequencing\n\n1. Finish Tier 1 performance/scalability work and publish the supported envelope.\n2. Establish snapshot-addressing and operational foundations.\n3. Deliver historical inspection and secure standalone access.\n4. Add data branching/merge workflows and broader application compatibility.\n5. Harden long-lived operation and publish a standalone deployment profile.\n\nChild stories remain BACKLOG until explicitly planned and specified.\n\n## Critical paths\n\nThe strict version-control critical path is:\n\n```\nrdb-7b1006  Snapshot addressing\n    ↓\nrdb-33692c  Named data branches\n    ↓\nrdb-23dce8  Safe branch switching\n    ↓\nrdb-e35ef0  Branch merging\n    ↓\nrdb-9bbd62  Commit revert\n```\n\nParallel after `rdb-7b1006`:\n\n- `rdb-ce14fb` — History/log\n- `rdb-71f167` — Historical queries\n- `rdb-493758` — Row/schema diffs\n- `rdb-2483e8` — Detached historical checkout\n\nOther critical tracks:\n\n```\nSecure server:\nrdb-d0b39d → rdb-5720c1 → rdb-8d63a3\nconfiguration → auth/TLS → authorization\n\nApplication compatibility:\nrdb-a2d28e ─┐\n             ├→ rdb-65b203 → rdb-8b1539\nrdb-48af2f ─┘\nAUTO_INCREMENT + foreign keys → metadata → compatibility profile\n```\n\nThe best first post-performance issue is `rdb-7b1006`; it unlocks nearly the entire differentiated version-control track.","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T15:50:55.738711Z","created_by":"codex-mcp-client/0.158.0 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
