{"id":"rdb-cf3eab","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-1d1738aa-6298-41f9-a2eb-99df86d657cd","text":"Ready for review.\n\n**Run:** all four modes at `c1aa399`, clean tree, one `dbbench` binary, run sequentially with only the system under test up. Wall time: native-git 11m49s, journal 9m09s (50 min last time), MySQL 10.6 s, Dolt 13 s. All four JSONs record `c1aa399…`, an empty `working_tree_status`, suite v1 and the default config.\n\n**Correctness:** MySQL, journal and native-git passed every check. Dolt 2.3.5 failed again, with lost updates at 4 clients and serialization errors at 16. This time no fixture stopped early, so every cell is measured.\n\n**Published:**\n- The previous latest.md and its JSONs moved to `history/2026-09-28-57a0cd8-scorecard{.md,/}`, with only the banner and link fixes changed.\n- The new latest.md tables are generated by a render script. The script was first checked by reproducing the old latest.md tables exactly. latest.md also has a new \"Changes since `57a0cd8`\" section.\n- README table refreshed and checked against the JSON by script. It gains Range (100) and Full scan rows, and the prose is updated: range reads are sub-ms, and journal merge sync is ~3 s instead of ~20 s.\n\n**Headline changes at 50k rows**\n- Range(100): 87–89 ms → 0.13–0.14 ms.\n- Full scan: 87–89 ms → 32–33 ms (MySQL 26, Dolt 39).\n- Journal divergent merge: 19.8 → 2.6 s; conflict resolve: 20.7 → 3.0 s.\n- Journal peak RSS: 604 → 360 MiB.\n\n**Notes**\n- `ordered_limit` in dbbench is `ORDER BY id DESC`, so it still sorts the whole table (37–39 ms) until reverse scans land (rdb-acd36d). latest.md says so.\n- MySQL itself moved up to 1.7× between runs on small p50s. Only RepoDB changes well above that are claimed.\n- rdb-59c7d7: the regression it tracks (journal merge 8.5 s → 19.8 s) now measures 2.6 s. It can probably be closed; I left it for you to decide."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:18:16.344726Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-df092b","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Local write coordination for concurrent sessions (journal mode)","description":"Coordinate concurrent local writers, instead of exposing repository publication conflicts directly to SQL applications. This is the main remaining performance gap for standalone-server use. Reads, scans and sync are no longer the bottleneck (scorecard at `c1aa399`, rdb-cf3eab).\n\n## Evidence (scorecard `c1aa399`, 50k rows)\n\n- Mixed 80/20 read/write, journal: 291 ops/s at 4 clients and 195 ops/s at 16, against MySQL's 5,272 and 7,562 (18× and 39× lower). This is despite RepoDB running embedded while MySQL goes over loopback TCP.\n- Contended increment, journal: 157 and 128 ops/s against 2,227 and 2,370, with 19% and 43% of attempts rejected.\n- **False conflicts.** In the mixed workload every client updates its own row (`experiments/dbbench/main.go`), yet 6–9% of attempts are rejected. `CommitTypedEdits` rejects any transaction whose base generation isn't current (`common/repository/working.go`), so conflicts are repository-wide, not per row.\n- **Serialized fsync.** Each journal commit takes the lock and does its own `fsync` (~5 ms), which caps commits at roughly 200/s whatever the conflict rate.\n\nDolt is not a valid correctness baseline for these workloads: its acknowledged updates fail verification.\n\n## Goals\n\n- Disjoint writers don't conflict.\n- Concurrent commits share durability work.\n- Same-row contention behaves like a MySQL server: bounded waits, safe retries, and standard error codes.\n- Snapshot isolation is preserved. Arbitrary multi-statement transactions are never replayed invisibly.\n\n## Out of scope\n\nNative-git mode stays positioned as audit-first (\"every transaction is a snapshot\"), not as the high-concurrency server mode.\n\n## Children\n\n1. Per-key write conflict detection with commit rebase\n2. Group commit for the journal\n3. Automatic retry for single-statement autocommit transactions\n4. MySQL-compatible conflict errors, lock-wait timeouts and fair commit ordering\n\nSuggested order: 1 and 2 first (they are independent), then 3, which depends on 1, and 4.\n\nThe standalone server should not claim general OLTP concurrency until this epic lands.","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"rdb-4c3ae3","sort_order":"aY","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-df092b","target_id":"rdb-cf94d8","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-df092b","target_id":"rdb-cf3eab","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["epic","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:24:50.209724Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-40ba7c","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Per-key write conflict detection with commit rebase (journal)","description":"Today `CommitTypedEdits` returns `ErrConflict` whenever the journal generation moved since the transaction's base snapshot, even if the concurrent commits touched unrelated rows. In the scorecard's mixed workload each client writes its own row, and still 6–9% of attempts are rejected.\n\n## Direction\n\nAt commit, under the journal lock:\n- If the base is stale, compare the transaction's written keys (table + primary key, per `TypedTableEdit`) with the keys written by the transactions committed since its base.\n- If they are disjoint, rebase: apply the typed edits onto the current state and commit. Otherwise reject with a conflict. This is first-committer-wins snapshot isolation.\n\n## Must handle\n\n- **Secondary and unique indexes.** Re-derive index edits against the new base, and re-validate unique constraints. Two disjoint rows inserting the same unique value must still conflict.\n- **Schema changes.** A transaction that changes a schema, or whose base schema differs from the current one, still conflicts, as does a dropped table.\n- **Reads are not tracked.** This gives snapshot isolation, not serializability, the same guarantee as today for committed transactions. Document the write-skew implications.\n- **Native-git mode** is out of scope.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The mixed workload shows 0% rejections, because its writers are disjoint.\n- Same-row writers still conflict.\n- A test proves that unique-index collisions across disjoint rows are rejected.\n- A crash during a rebased commit recovers like any other journal commit.","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"rdb-df092b","sort_order":"aZ","labels":["feature","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:25:05.235821Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-8f75fc","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Group commit for the journal","description":"Each journal commit takes the working-state lock and does its own `fsync` (~5 ms at p50). Commits are therefore serialized at roughly 200/s whatever the conflict rate, which bounds the scorecard's mixed and contended throughput (291/195 and 157/128 ops/s at 50k rows).\n\n## Direction\n\n- Commits that arrive while an `fsync` is in flight join the next batch.\n- One leader writes the batch's prepare/commit records and does a single `fsync`, and every member is acknowledged only after that `fsync`.\n- Durability semantics are unchanged: no commit is acknowledged before its records are durable.\n- The batch is bounded, both in maximum size and in a maximum wait of about zero when there is no concurrency, so single-client latency does not regress.\n\nThis interacts with per-key conflict detection (sibling issue): batch members must be validated against earlier members of the same batch.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Crash tests cover a kill during a batched `fsync`: no acknowledged commit is lost, and no unacknowledged commit is partially visible.\n- Single-client update p50 does not regress.\n- Journal mixed and contended throughput rises measurably at 4 and 16 clients. Record it in the next scorecard.","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"rdb-df092b","sort_order":"aa","labels":["feature","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:25:05.261593Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-eb6674","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Automatic retry for single-statement autocommit transactions on conflict","description":"After per-key conflict detection, genuine same-row conflicts remain. The scorecard's contended increment rejects 19–43% of journal attempts at 50k rows.\n\n## Direction\n\nWhen a conflict hits an **implicit, single-statement autocommit** transaction (for example `UPDATE counter SET value = value + 1 WHERE id = 1`), re-execute the statement against a fresh snapshot, with a bounded number of attempts and backoff. The client never saw intermediate state, so re-running the statement is equivalent to it having arrived later.\n\n## Never retry\n\n- explicit `BEGIN … COMMIT` transactions;\n- multi-statement transactions;\n- statements with non-deterministic or externally visible side effects (to be defined: e.g. `NOW()` and `RAND()` are fine to re-evaluate; anything that returned rows to the client before the conflict is not).\n\nThese fail with the error defined in the MySQL-compatible errors sibling issue.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The contended-increment workload in autocommit mode ends with the correct final value, and with rejections only after the retry budget is exhausted.\n- Explicit transactions are never retried (tested).","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"rdb-df092b","sort_order":"ab","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-eb6674","target_id":"rdb-40ba7c","kind":"depends_on"}],"labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:25:17.552603Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-1d7fab","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"MySQL-compatible conflict errors, lock-wait timeouts and fair commit ordering","description":"A write conflict currently surfaces as RepoDB's own `ErrConflict` (\"RepoDB data head changed\"). Standard MySQL clients and ORMs recognize deadlock/serialization errors and retry them. They don't recognize this one.\n\n## Direction\n\n- **Error mapping.** Map commit conflicts to MySQL error 1213 (`ER_LOCK_DEADLOCK`, SQLSTATE 40001) on the MySQL-protocol server, and expose an equivalent typed error in the embedded API, so client retry logic works unchanged.\n- **Lock-wait timeouts.** Waiting for the journal lock gets a bounded, configurable timeout (analogous to `innodb_lock_wait_timeout`, error 1205) instead of an unbounded wait.\n- **Fair ordering.** Waiting committers are served roughly in arrival order, so no session starves under sustained contention.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A `go-sql-driver/mysql` client sees 1213/40001 on a conflict.\n- A lock-wait timeout returns 1205.\n- A starvation test under 16-client contention shows bounded per-session wait.","status":"BACKLOG","parent_id":"rdb-df092b","sort_order":"ac","labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:25:17.578463Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-cf3eab","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-47aade3c-b0be-41ff-b62c-1dbc88c6f34e","text":"Review fix: the README and latest.md said conflicting writers to *the same rows* are rejected. In fact both modes reject any write transaction whose base is no longer the head, even when the rows written are disjoint (`working.go` generation check; native-git compares the data head). Both now say so. latest.md cites the mixed workload, where clients each write their own row, yet 6–15% of attempts are rejected at 50k rows. Follow-up work is filed as epic rdb-df092b."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:25:37.763707Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"dbbench: keep Git auto-gc out of timed workloads","description":"Git's background auto-gc and maintenance run inside the fixture repositories while dbbench is timing workloads, and the resulting stalls land on whichever workload happens to be running. Comparing the `57a0cd8` and `c1aa399` scorecards (rdb-cf3eab), native-git at 50k rows:\n\n| Workload | 57a0cd8 (min / q1 / median / q3 / max ms) | c1aa399 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Update x1 | 165 / 245 / 323 / 552 / **1,833** | 151 / 157 / 199 / 228 / 467 |\n| Update x10 | 181 / 210 / 258 / 298 / 931 | 153 / 182 / 268 / 469 / **1,832** |\n| Insert | 149 / 157 / 161 / 164 / 166 | 147 / 153 / 155 / 157 / 164 |\n\nThe ~1.8 s stalls moved from Update x1 to Update x10, so both workloads' p50 and p95 moved by ±1.6× with no code change behind it. Insert and delete use the same publication path with tight distributions and stayed flat. rdb-802f5d already had to make the size walk tolerate files that auto-gc prunes mid-walk (`directoryBytes`, `experiments/dbbench/main.go`).\n\n## Direction\n\n- Set `gc.auto=0` and `maintenance.auto=false` in every fixture repository dbbench creates or clones: the working repo (`git init`), the bare remotes (`init --bare`) and the clones.\n- Run `git gc` explicitly, untimed, at fixed points (for example after bulk load and between workload groups), so object-store state stays comparable across runs and modes.\n- Record the gc policy in the JSON report (for example `\"git_gc\": \"manual\"`), and state it in benchmark.md's methodology.\n- Auto-gc stalls are a real cost for native-git users. If that cost should be measured, add a separate, explicit workload for it, rather than letting it land randomly on others.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Two consecutive native-git runs at the same revision show update x1, update x10, insert and delete p50s within about 10% of each other, with no multi-second max outliers.\n- The report records the gc policy, and benchmark.md documents it.\n- The link to directoryBytes' tolerance stays, since it's harmless.","status":"BACKLOG","sort_order":"ad","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-2124bc","target_id":"rdb-cf3eab","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-2124bc","target_id":"rdb-802f5d","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["task","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:28:04.333074Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"Native-git workloads show multi-second stalls that land on whichever workload happens to be running. The most likely cause is Git's background auto-gc and maintenance running inside the fixture repositories during timed requests. This is not confirmed yet. Comparing the `57a0cd8` and `c1aa399` scorecards (rdb-cf3eab), native-git at 50k rows:\n\n| Workload | 57a0cd8 (min / q1 / median / q3 / max ms) | c1aa399 |\n|---|---|---|\n| Update x1 | 165 / 245 / 323 / 552 / **1,833** | 151 / 157 / 199 / 228 / 467 |\n| Update x10 | 181 / 210 / 258 / 298 / 931 | 153 / 182 / 268 / 469 / **1,832** |\n| Insert | 149 / 157 / 161 / 164 / 166 | 147 / 153 / 155 / 157 / 164 |\n\nThe ~1.8 s stalls moved from Update x1 to Update x10, so both workloads' p50 and p95 moved by up to 1.6× with no code change behind it. Insert and delete use the same publication path, have tight distributions, and stayed flat. Auto-gc is already known to run during benchmarks: rdb-802f5d had to make the size walk tolerate files it prunes mid-walk (`directoryBytes`, `experiments/dbbench/main.go`).\n\n## Direction\n\n1. **Confirm the cause.** Log `git gc --auto` and maintenance activity (e.g. `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT`, or watch for `gc.pid` and new packs) against the stalled requests.\n2. If confirmed, set `gc.auto=0` and `maintenance.auto=false` in every fixture repository dbbench creates or clones: the working repo, the bare remotes and the clones.\n3. Run `git gc` explicitly and untimed at fixed points (for example after bulk load and between workload groups), so object-store state stays comparable across runs and modes.\n4. Record the gc policy in the JSON report (for example `\"git_gc\": \"manual\"`) and in benchmark.md's methodology.\n5. Auto-gc stalls are a real cost for native-git users. If that cost should be measured, add a separate, explicit workload for it, rather than letting it land at random on others.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The cause of the stalls is identified. If it is not gc, update this issue before changing the harness.\n- Two consecutive native-git runs at the same revision show update x1, update x10, insert and delete p50s within about 10% of each other, with no multi-second max outliers.\n- The report and benchmark.md state the gc policy.\n- `directoryBytes` keeps its tolerance for vanishing files.","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:28:22.990644Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-cf3eab","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-64dc849c-9eb4-4115-b595-70e16e2c98ec","text":"Review follow-up: the README scope sentence no longer says \"small tool databases\". It now states the positioning (local-first, version-controlled relational workloads) and bounds the claims to the measured sizes (up to 50k rows):\n- sub-ms point and indexed-range reads;\n- full scans in tens of ms;\n- 100-row batches under 10 ms in journal mode;\n- cross-clone merges in 2–3 s;\n- concurrent writers named as the main limitation.\n\nEvery figure is checked against latest.md. Also filed rdb-2124bc: native-git Update x1's apparent 319 → 196 ms gain is a stall that moved to Update x10, most likely auto-gc, so it is not claimed anywhere."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T16:28:23.017751Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
