{"id":"rdb-931f20","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"dependencies":null,"labels":null,"sort_order":"a0","status":"DOING"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T19:06:57.98563Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-8f75fc","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-d21d7312-cbb6-4e5f-9b30-3c70d3f31d15","text":"Correction to this issue's premise, found while documenting (rdb-931f20): a group-commit primitive already exists. `WorkingState.groupSync` (`common/repository/working.go`) batches concurrent `fsync` calls under a leader. It can't take effect, though: `CommitTypedEdits` (and `Commit`) acquire `w.lock()` (the in-process `w.mu` plus the `working.lock` file lock) before validation and keep it through `appendRecords` → `groupSync`. At most one committer per process can be inside `groupSync`, so every batch has size 1.\n\nThe work is therefore mostly lock restructuring, not a new primitive:\n- validate and append under the lock;\n- release the lock, or hand off ordering, before the flush;\n- acknowledge only after the covering `fsync`.\n\nThe replay and recovery ordering guarantees (matched prepare/commit pairs, generation order) must hold when appends from several transactions share one flush. Cross-process committers serialize on `working.lock` regardless."},"created_at":"2026-09-28T19:08:18.114062Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-e0c717","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"rdb-e0c717","target_id":"rdb-931f20"}],"description":"Found by code reading while documenting (rdb-931f20); not yet reproduced.\n\n## The race\n\n`integration.Sync` checks that the journal is clean (`requireCleanWorkingState`, `integration/integration.go`) before fetching and once more after fetching. It does not hold the journal lock (`working.lock`) from that check until it advances `refs/repodb/data` under the publication lock:\n- `FastForwardSnapshot` when the local side is behind;\n- the merge publication paths.\n\nA journal-mode transaction that commits in that window appends a record based on the old head. The window includes validating the fetched snapshot (`repo.SnapshotAt` + `engine.ValidateSnapshot`), which takes seconds at 50k rows.\n\n## Consequence\n\nAfter sync moves the head, the journal is dirty and based on a commit that is no longer the head. `WorkingState.reconcileHead` then returns `ErrWorkingBaseChanged` on every load, unless the manifests happen to be equal. As a result:\n- journal-mode engines can't run transactions or checkpoint;\n- native-git engines refuse to open because the journal is dirty;\n- no command recovers from the state.\n\nThe acknowledged journal writes are durable but stranded. No test covers `ErrWorkingBaseChanged`.\n\n## Fix direction\n\n- One lock-order protocol shared by journal saves, checkpoint and sync. For example, sync acquires `working.lock` before the publication lock, re-checks cleanliness under it, and holds it through the head update. Keep fetch and validation outside the lock where possible, re-validating the head under the locks.\n- Define a recovery path for a repository already in this state: rebase the pending journal edits onto the new head, or checkpoint them as a divergent commit and merge.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A test interleaves a journal commit between sync's clean check and its head update (a fault injector or hook), and shows that either the commit or the sync is rejected. The repository never ends in `ErrWorkingBaseChanged`.\n- A documented recovery exists for repositories already wedged.","labels":["bug"],"sort_order":"af","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Sync can advance the data head under a concurrent journal commit, wedging working state"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T19:11:43.994701Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-c56142","type":"CREATE","payload":{"dependencies":[{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-515fae"},{"kind":"depends_on","source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-e0c717"},{"kind":"relates_to","source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-931f20"}],"description":"`engine.Open` already defaults to journal persistence, but `repodb start` and `repodb-server` default to native-git. For almost every workload journal is the better mode. At 50k rows (latest.md, `c1aa399`):\n- single-row writes take ~5 ms versus 155–260 ms in native-git;\n- 100-row batches take under 10 ms versus 150–240 ms;\n- mixed and contended throughput is 10–40× higher.\n\nMake journal the default everywhere, and keep native-git as an explicit opt-in \"audit mode\" in which every SQL transaction is a Git data commit.\n\n## Depends on\n\n- **rdb-515fae — journal retention and compaction.** The journal is never compacted today and grows for the life of the repository. A default mode needs bounded disk use.\n- **rdb-e0c717 — sync/journal race.** Sync can currently advance the data head under a concurrent journal commit and wedge working state. A default mode must not.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Defaults.** `repodb start --persistence` and `repodb-server --persistence` default to `journal`. `native-git` remains selectable and is documented as audit mode: every transaction lands in `git log refs/repodb/data`, the Git repository alone is a complete backup, and there is no checkpoint step.\n- **Backup guidance.** Journal mode needs the Git repository and `\u003cgit-common-dir\u003e/repodb/working/v1/journal` copied at one consistent point; copying only Git loses uncheckpointed work. Provide a supported way to do that (for example `repodb commit` before backup, or a backup command if rdb-f33cb0 lands first), and make docs/cli.md state it prominently.\n- **Non-interactive sync.** Today `repodb sync` prompts to checkpoint a dirty journal only when stdin is a terminal; otherwise it fails with `ErrWorkingDirty`. Define explicit non-interactive behavior, for example `repodb sync --checkpoint -m \u003cmessage\u003e` to checkpoint and sync in one step, while failing by default with an actionable message. Scripts and CI need a documented path.\n- **Mode switching.** Existing repositories used in native-git mode open in journal mode without migration: an absent journal means clean. Switching back to native-git requires a checkpoint, as today. Document both directions.\n- **Docs.** README, docs/cli.md, docs/library.md and docs/architecture.md describe journal as the default and native-git as audit mode.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The server and CLI default to journal; tests cover both explicit modes and the default.\n- Non-interactive sync behavior is tested with and without the new flag.\n- Backup guidance is documented and exercised by a test (backup, restore, verify uncheckpointed rows survive).\n- The next scorecard's README table and summary reflect the default.","labels":["feature"],"sort_order":"ag","status":"BACKLOG","title":"Make journal the default persistence for server and CLI"},"created_at":"2026-09-28T19:11:57.176882Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.283 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
