{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DOING","sort_order":"a0","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:12:09.703658Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-436f8253-782c-4748-8178-a4b0e4a21db3","text":"## Step 1 result: the update stalls are **not** auto-gc\n\n**Run.** `dbbench -mode native-git -rows 50000` (default clients and requests) at main `4b66f4a`, with `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT` capturing every Git process (RepoDB's included): 20,295 processes over 285 s. Fixtures were on the system disk (scratchpad), not the scorecard volume.\n\n**Symptom reproduced.** `update_batch_10` had p50 268 ms but p95 1,122 ms and max 1,405 ms (requests 4 and 8). update x1 (max 434), x100 (476), insert (174) and delete (179) were tight.\n\n**Findings:**\n\n1. **No gc or maintenance ran during any non-sync timed workload.** Every `gc`/`maintenance`/`repack`/`commit-graph` process in the trace was spawned by `git fetch`, `git push` or `receive-pack` in the bare remote. RepoDB's native commit uses plumbing only (`ls-tree`, `rev-parse`, `hash-object -w`, `read-tree`, `update-index`, `write-tree`, `commit-tree`, `update-ref`), which never triggers auto-gc.\n2. **The stall time sits inside object-writing Git processes.** Slowest update commit (#45, ~1.4 s) vs its neighbour #44:\n\n   | | #44 | #45 |\n   |---|---|---|\n   | `hash-object -w` | 76 ms | **595 ms** |\n   | `write-tree` | 89 ms | **674 ms** |\n   | `commit-tree`, `update-ref` | ~7 ms | ~7 ms |\n\n   In-process gaps between Git calls were normal (5–44 ms). Across all 939 commits from t=11–119 s, `hash-object`/`write-tree` have p50 2–3 ms and p95 60–77 ms. Only 4 commits exceeded 300 ms, and in each both writers were slow together, clustered at t=18–28 s. `update-ref` and `commit-tree` never exceeded 15 ms. This pattern fits a transient system-wide write/fsync stall (both writers fsync objects with `core.fsync=committed`), not Git housekeeping. The source isn't identified: `log show` returned nothing for the window.\n3. **Auto-maintenance does affect the sync workloads.** After each `git fetch`, Git starts `maintenance run --auto` **detached**: `fetch` returns in 27–78 ms while the child runs 100–150 ms (once 1.6 s, a `repack -d -l`) and overlaps the next timed request. At t=119 s, the push measured in the sync workload took 938 ms while that repack ran. Receive-pack in the bare remotes also runs `maintenance --auto`.\n\n**Implication for the direction in this issue.** Setting `gc.auto=0` / `maintenance.auto=false` in the fixtures will **not** remove the update-workload outliers. It would remove housekeeping noise from the sync workloads (`sync_unchanged`, `edit_sync_roundtrip`, `divergent_merge`, `conflict_resolve`), where it is real.\n\nPer the acceptance criteria, no harness change has been made. Waiting for a decision on scope."},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:19:36.639551Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-c85855","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Investigate transient fsync stalls in native-git commits (hash-object/write-tree 10× slower)","description":"Split out of rdb-2124bc, whose trace showed that the native-git update outliers are **not** Git auto-gc.\n\n## Evidence (rdb-2124bc comment, `GIT_TRACE2_EVENT` over a full 50k native-git dbbench run)\n\n- The stalled commit's time sits inside the two object-writing Git processes. `hash-object -w` took 595 ms (normal ~2 ms p50, 60 ms p95) and `write-tree` 674 ms (normal ~3 / 77 ms) in the same commit. `commit-tree` and `update-ref` stayed under 15 ms, and RepoDB's in-process gaps were normal.\n- Only 4 of 939 commits exceeded 300 ms. Each time both writers were slow together, clustered in a ~10 s window. No Git housekeeping ran then.\n- The pattern fits a transient system-wide write/fsync stall. Git runs with `core.fsync=committed,reference`, `core.fsyncMethod=fsync`.\n\n## Questions\n\n- What stalls: APFS flush, Spotlight/`mds`, Time Machine local snapshots, memory pressure, or the loose-object directory layout as loose objects accumulate?\n- Does it reproduce on the scorecard volume (`/Volumes/Work`) and on Linux?\n- Is it a real user-facing cost of native-git mode? Each commit writes many loose objects with fsync. If so, could RepoDB write fewer objects per commit, for example by writing a pack instead of loose objects?\n\n## Direction\n\n1. Repeat the traced run with `fs_usage -w -f filesys` (needs sudo) or `sample`/Instruments over the stall window, and collect `log show` with sufficient privileges.\n2. Compare loose-object counts at stall time against non-stall commits.\n3. Report a cause, and either a fix (RepoDB or harness) or a documented caveat in `docs/benchmark.md`.","status":"BACKLOG","sort_order":"ae","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-c85855","target_id":"rdb-2124bc","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["task","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:20:42.163205Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"dbbench: keep Git auto-maintenance out of timed workloads (sync)","description":"Originally filed as \"keep Git auto-gc out of timed workloads\", suspecting gc behind the native-git update outliers. Step 1 (trace evidence in the comments) showed:\n\n- **Update outliers are not gc.** No gc or maintenance ran during any non-sync timed workload. The stall time sits inside `hash-object`/`write-tree` (transient fsync stalls). That investigation moved to rdb-c85855.\n- **Auto-maintenance does land in the timed sync workloads.** After each `git fetch` (and in receive-pack in the bare remotes), Git starts `maintenance run --auto` **detached**. It runs 100 ms–1.6 s (`repack -d -l`) and overlaps the next timed request: 615 maintenance processes in one 50k native-git run, one overlapping a measured push (938 ms).\n\n## Scope (rescoped with the user, 2026-10-01)\n\n1. Every repository dbbench creates (local, peer, bare remotes, sync pairs) sets `gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false` and `receive.autogc=false`.\n2. `git gc --quiet` runs untimed in the local repository and the remote: after `initial_publish_sync`, and before the concurrency, wire and sync workload groups.\n3. The report records `\"git_gc\": \"manual\"`, and `docs/benchmark.md` states the policy.\n4. `directoryBytes` keeps its tolerance for vanishing files.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A traced run shows no `maintenance` or `gc --auto` process, only the explicit `gc` calls.\n- Two consecutive native-git 50k runs complete, with sync workloads free of housekeeping overlap.\n- `docs/benchmark.md` states the gc policy and notes that native-git write outliers can come from fsync stalls (rdb-c85855).\n- `make test` and `make lint` pass.","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:22:25.919536Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:22:25.952472Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-2124bc","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-bb64c547-3349-42ab-a5d3-80b1439e6d3f","text":"Ready for review (rescoped as agreed; the I/O stalls are in rdb-c85855).\n\n**Change** (`experiments/dbbench/main.go`):\n- `configureFixture` sets `gc.auto=0`, `maintenance.auto=false` and `receive.autogc=false` in every repository the harness creates: local, peer, bare remotes, and sync-pair remotes and clones.\n- `gitGC` runs `git gc --quiet` untimed in the local repository and the remote, after `initial_publish_sync` and before the concurrency, wire and sync groups.\n- The report records `\"git_gc\": \"manual\"`.\n- `directoryBytes` is unchanged and keeps its tolerance for vanishing files.\n\n**Verification:**\n- Traced smoke runs in both modes: **0** `maintenance` and **0** `gc --auto` processes (baseline: 615 maintenance processes per 50k run), with only the 8 explicit `gc` calls.\n- Two consecutive native-git 50k runs (2, 3) vs the traced baseline (1). Both completed, git_gc = manual. Sync workloads, p50/p95/max in ms:\n\n| workload | run1 (before) | run2 | run3 |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| publish_sync_after_writes | 1192 | 380 | 404 |\n| edit_sync_roundtrip | 843/1202/1287 | 780/845/882 | 789/869/869 |\n| divergent_edit_merge | 1926/2116/2135 | 1848/2146/2149 | 1896/2188/2274 |\n| conflict_resolve_roundtrip | 2330/2760/2802 | 2116/2283/2366 | 2169/2275/2711 |\n| sync_unchanged | 156/170/172 | 131/145/147 | 214/262/293 |\n\nThe housekeeping overlap is gone (publish after writes fell 3×, round-trip p95 −30%). `sync_unchanged` still varies between runs (131 vs 214 ms p50) with no housekeeping present: ordinary machine noise on 30 samples.\n\n- The update outliers persist as expected (run3 `update_batch_10` max 2.9 s), tracked in rdb-c85855.\n\n**Docs:** `benchmark.md` has a \"Git housekeeping\" paragraph (policy, why, and the fsync-stall caveat citing rdb-c85855) and lists `git_gc` among the report fields. `testing.md` lists the new harness test. `latest.md` is unchanged; the next scorecard will carry the policy.\n\n**Test:** `TestFixturesDisableAutomaticGitHousekeeping`. `make test` and `make lint` pass."},"created_at":"2026-10-01T12:33:01.52688Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
