{"id":"rdb-4d677f","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Truncate the journal at checkpoint (bounded journal size)","description":"Minimal slice of rdb-515fae, pulled forward because journal persistence is already the library default and rdb-c56142 (journal default for CLI/server) needs bounded journal size.\n\n## Problem\n\n`\u003cgit-common-dir\u003e/repodb/working/v1/journal` is append-only and is never truncated. Every transaction and checkpoint record stays for the life of the repository. Replay already starts from the last checkpoint's base (lazy replay, rdb-92b9e2), so everything before the last `checkpoint` marker is dead weight on disk.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **At checkpoint**, while still holding `working.lock` (rdb-e0c717), rewrite the journal down to the new checkpoint marker: write a new file, fsync it, rename it over the old one, and fsync the directory. Crash-safe: a crash before the rename leaves the old, valid journal, and one after leaves the new one.\n- **Other open engines and processes.** They cache the journal's file identity and offset. A replaced file must be detected (the existing `os.SameFile` check) and trigger a full replay of the short new file. Cover the journal-dirty scan cache (`journalDirtyLocked`) the same way.\n- **`RecoverTransaction`.** It settles uncertain journal outcomes by transaction ID. Define what remains answerable after truncation: transactions included in a checkpoint are `committed` through their Git commit. Decide whether to keep the last checkpoint's transaction IDs (for example in the checkpoint record) or document that recovery after a checkpoint answers from Git history. No acknowledged transaction may be reported wrongly.\n- **Windows.** Rename over an open file uses the existing robustio retry path. Readers must not hold the journal open across operations.\n- **Docs.** `docs/architecture.md` (journal growth, which cites rdb-515fae) and `docs/cli.md` (backup notes) describe the bounded journal.\n\n## Out of scope (stays in rdb-515fae)\n\nConfigurable retention policy, archiving journal history, and a `repodb compact` command.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Across N checkpoints with writes in between, journal size stays bounded: it holds only the records after the last checkpoint plus the marker, and doesn't grow with N.\n- A second engine open on the same repository during truncation keeps working: it sees the new file and full-replays it correctly.\n- Crash or fault injection before and after the rename leaves a loadable journal with every acknowledged transaction.\n- `RecoverTransaction` behavior after truncation is tested and documented.\n- `make test` and `make lint` pass.","status":"PLANNED","parent_id":"rdb-515fae","sort_order":"a9","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-4d677f","target_id":"rdb-c56142","kind":"dependency_of"}],"labels":["feature"]},"created_at":"2026-10-01T13:39:46.706117Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-c56142","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"description":"`engine.Open` already defaults to journal persistence, but `repodb start` and `repodb-server` default to native-git. For almost every workload journal is the better mode. At 50k rows (latest.md, `c1aa399`):\n- single-row writes take ~5 ms versus 155–260 ms in native-git;\n- 100-row batches take under 10 ms versus 150–240 ms;\n- mixed and contended throughput is 10–40× higher.\n\nMake journal the default everywhere, and keep native-git as an explicit opt-in \"audit mode\" in which every SQL transaction is a Git data commit.\n\n## Depends on\n\n- **rdb-4d677f — truncate the journal at checkpoint.** A default mode needs bounded disk use. This is the minimal slice of rdb-515fae, pulled out of the lifecycle epic (2026-10-01). The retention policy, archiving and `repodb compact` stay in rdb-515fae and are not required here.\n- **rdb-e0c717 — sync/journal race.** Done (`4b66f4a`): the data head can no longer move under a dirty journal.\n\n## Scope\n\n- **Defaults.** `repodb start --persistence` and `repodb-server --persistence` default to `journal`. `native-git` remains selectable and is documented as audit mode: every transaction lands in `git log refs/repodb/data`, the Git repository alone is a complete backup, and there is no checkpoint step.\n- **Backup guidance.** Journal mode needs the Git repository and `\u003cgit-common-dir\u003e/repodb/working/v1/journal` copied at one consistent point; copying only Git loses uncheckpointed work. Provide a supported way to do that (for example `repodb commit` before backup, or a backup command if rdb-f33cb0 lands first), and make docs/cli.md state it prominently.\n- **Non-interactive sync.** Today `repodb sync` prompts to checkpoint a dirty journal only when stdin is a terminal; otherwise it fails with `ErrWorkingDirty`. Define explicit non-interactive behavior, for example `repodb sync --checkpoint -m \u003cmessage\u003e` to checkpoint and sync in one step, while failing by default with an actionable message. Scripts and CI need a documented path.\n- **Mode switching.** Existing repositories used in native-git mode open in journal mode without migration: an absent journal means clean. Switching back to native-git requires a checkpoint, as today. Document both directions.\n- **Docs.** README, docs/cli.md, docs/library.md and docs/architecture.md describe journal as the default and native-git as audit mode.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- The server and CLI default to journal; tests cover both explicit modes and the default.\n- Non-interactive sync behavior is tested with and without the new flag.\n- Backup guidance is documented and exercised by a test (backup, restore, verify uncheckpointed rows survive).\n- The next scorecard's README table and summary reflect the default.","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-4d677f","kind":"depends_on"},{"source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-e0c717","kind":"depends_on"},{"source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-515fae","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-c56142","target_id":"rdb-931f20","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-01T13:40:05.643575Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-515fae","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-a75a4b05-87ae-40d6-8683-ddfbcdff02bb","text":"Split on 2026-10-01: the minimal slice, automatic journal truncation at checkpoint (bounded size), is now rdb-4d677f (PLANNED, child of this issue). It was pulled forward because journal is already the library default and rdb-c56142 needs bounded disk use. This issue keeps the configurable retention policy, archiving of journal history and the `repodb compact` command, and stays in the lifecycle epic."},"created_at":"2026-10-01T13:40:05.672357Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-59c7d7","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-2765c354-4bdd-49b9-9d2e-e05f3affcec1","text":"Closing: resolved by rdb-92b9e2 (lazy journal replay), as the earlier comment predicted. The scorecard refresh at `c1aa399` (rdb-cf3eab, `docs/benchmarks/latest.md`) includes that fix.\n\nJournal mode, 50k rows, p50:\n\n| Workload | 2026-09-13 (baseline) | `57a0cd8` (regression) | `c1aa399` (latest) |\n| --- | ---: | ---: | ---: |\n| Sync unchanged | 682 ms | 953 ms | 423 ms |\n| Edit/sync roundtrip | 2,491 ms | 3,807 ms | 1,167 ms |\n| Divergent merge | 8,541 ms | 19,818 ms | 2,567 ms |\n| Conflict resolve | 9,218 ms | 20,721 ms | 3,001 ms |\n\nEvery workload is now faster than the 2026-09-13 baseline it was compared against (divergent merge 3.3× faster), not just back to it. latest.md attributes the drop to rdb-92b9e2: cold replay had loaded a snapshot for every historical checkpoint, so sync cost grew with history. The remaining per-sync overhead (Git subprocess count and in-process work) is tracked in rdb-2c93ca.\n\nNo code change was needed."},"created_at":"2026-10-01T13:40:05.700881Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
