{"id":"rdb-4d677f","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-ff48e14f-9db0-4fd8-ae88-57b8cc66208b","text":"Ready for review.\n\n**What it does.** Each checkpoint, still under `working.lock`, rewrites the journal to one **anchor** frame (the checkpoint record plus the IDs of the transactions it replaces). The rewrite is crash-safe: temp file + fsync, rename, directory fsync. A replay starting with an anchor continues from its generation. Compaction is best effort: the checkpoint never fails because of it, the old journal stays valid, and `JournalCompactionFailures` counts failures.\n\n**Cache identity.** Both journal caches also check the file's first frame header, so a replaced journal that reuses the old inode number triggers a full replay. Without that check, the inode-reuse test fails with `invalid frame magic`.\n\n**`RecoverTransaction`.** Transaction IDs are now `\u003cgeneration\u003e-\u003crandom\u003e`. The anchor keeps the replaced interval's IDs, so answers are exact for the current and previous checkpoint interval. Older IDs get `unknown` with `ErrWorkingHistoryTruncated`, never a wrong `rejected`.\n\n**Tests:**\n- `engine/journal_compaction_test.go`:\n  - bounded size over 20 checkpoints × 50 inserts (1 frame each time, flat size, generations continue after reopen);\n  - a second engine following compactions with no false conflicts;\n  - simulated inode reuse;\n  - faults before and after the rename (checkpoint succeeds, journal loadable, next checkpoint compacts);\n  - `RecoverTransaction` across compactions.\n- `common/repository/journal_compact_internal_test.go`: prepared-without-commit and the generation ranges.\n- No existing test needed changes. `make test` and `make lint` pass, and the race detector passes on the journal concurrency tests.\n\n**Cost.** Checkpoints go from 180–190 ms to 185–201 ms (`BenchmarkJournalCheckpoint`).\n\n**Docs:**\n- `architecture.md`: state table, transaction ID format, replay identity, checkpoint step 4, growth, recovery;\n- `library.md`: `RecoverTransaction` after checkpoints;\n- `cli.md`: backup note;\n- `testing.md`: coverage.\n\nSeen but out of scope: `BenchmarkJournalCheckpoint/batch=10` takes ~1.1 s against ~0.19 s for batch=1 and batch=100, both before and after this change."},"created_at":"2026-10-01T15:02:17.720968Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-4d677f","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-10-01T15:02:43.896658Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-fb3d12","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Prolly chunk boundaries aren't content-local: small edits rewrite up to half the tree","description":"Found while investigating `BenchmarkJournalCheckpoint/batch=10` taking ~1.1 s against ~0.19 s for batch=1 and batch=100, during rdb-4d677f. The slowness predates that issue.\n\n## Symptom\n\nCheckpoint (and every native-git commit) time follows the number of new Git objects, which swings with the edit set rather than its size. A 10k-row table, journal checkpoint after updating keys 1..k:\n\n| keys updated | new loose objects | checkpoint |\n|---:|---:|---:|\n| 1 | 12 | 193 ms |\n| 2 | 132 | 892 ms |\n| 5 | 22 | 248 ms |\n| 10 | 175 | 1,135 ms |\n| 20 | 122 | 835 ms |\n| 50 | 231 | 1,536 ms |\n| 100 | 13 | 199 ms |\n\nDirectly in `common/prolly` (10k entries, 127 leaves, `DefaultOptions`), `Apply` writes **28–130 nodes** for 1–100 updated keys. A Prolly tree should rewrite one leaf per touched region plus its path. The time goes into `git hash-object -w` and `git write-tree` (483 ms and 505 ms instead of 29 ms and 37 ms in the slow checkpoint), which fsync every new object.\n\n## Cause\n\n`common/prolly/tree.go`:\n- `entryFingerprint` hashes **key and value**, so a value update changes the boundary decision.\n- The boundary hash **accumulates since the last cut** (`rolling = rotl(rolling, 1) ^ fingerprint`, reset to 0 at each cut) instead of being local to the current entry or a fixed window. Once one boundary moves, every later boundary depends on the new start. `Apply` keeps re-chunking until a new cut happens to land on an old boundary, which takes about one average chunk size worth of chunks (~65 leaves here).\n- `linkFingerprint` hashes the child **hash**, so internal-node boundaries move whenever a child's content changes.\n\n## Fix direction (verified by a throwaway experiment, since reverted)\n\nMake boundaries content-local:\n- a leaf boundary is decided by a hash of the current entry's **key** only;\n- an internal boundary by the child's **MaxKey** only;\n- no accumulation; keep min/max chunk sizes.\n\nWith that, `Apply` wrote **7–8 nodes for every batch size, 1 to 100 keys**, instead of 28–130.\n- `chunk()`, `leafChunker` and `SortedBuilder` must share the rule. History-independence tests (Apply equals a fresh Build) must keep passing.\n- It changes every tree's shape, so it's a **storage format change** (format v4). Alpha needs no migration; a format bump keeps clones consistent, since merge compares roots.\n- Re-measure `BenchmarkJournalCheckpoint`, `BenchmarkSQLWriteBatches` (native-git) and dbbench update workloads.\n- Check whether min/max size cuts, which still depend on position, need a resync bound in `Apply`.\n\n## Likely related\n\nrdb-c85855 (native-git \"fsync stalls\" in dbbench `update_batch_10`) has the same signature: `hash-object` and `write-tree` both ~10× slower in the same commit, nothing else slow. It's probably this bug, not system I/O. Re-check after the fix.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `Apply` for k updated keys writes O(k × depth) nodes, not O(chunk size × depth). A test bounds the nodes written for single-key updates at several positions.\n- Checkpoint benchmark times are flat across batch=1/10/100.\n- Format version bumped; docs (`architecture.md` Prolly section) updated.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a9","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-fb3d12","target_id":"rdb-4d677f","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-fb3d12","target_id":"rdb-c85855","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-01T15:07:35.623885Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
