{"id":"rdb-a0d510","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Tune Prolly chunk size after content-local boundaries (bulk publish writes ~20% more objects)","description":"Found in the rdb-13fbd8 scorecard refresh: journal \"initial publish + sync\" at 50k rows went 7.4 s (`c1aa399`) → 8.3–9.0 s.\n\nBisected with a probe (checkpoint right after a 50k-row journal bulk load, 3 runs each):\n- `0c6fa21` and `db2bdbd`: 5.1–5.3 s. Compaction (rdb-4d677f) is not the cause.\n- `c81d57a` (rdb-fb3d12): 5.9–6.3 s.\n\n**Cause.** The content-local boundary rule (key-only hash, cut when `size ≥ 32` and the low 6 bits are zero, or at 128) gives smaller chunks for the engine's binary BIGINT keys: the 50k-row `bench` table has **767 tree nodes (65 rows/node) instead of 638 (78 rows/node)**. A bulk publish writes and fsyncs ~20 % more Git objects.\n\n**Direction.** Tune `prolly.DefaultOptions` (for example `BoundaryBits` 7, or a larger `MinChunkEntries`) for a target average chunk size, and check how evenly FNV-64a's low bits spread over the encoded key formats. Weigh against incremental-edit cost (one leaf per update either way), read cost per node, and the edit-locality test bounds. Changing the options changes tree shapes, so it's a storage format bump.","status":"BACKLOG","sort_order":"ae","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-a0d510","target_id":"rdb-fb3d12","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-a0d510","target_id":"rdb-13fbd8","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["task","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T06:13:20.521717Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-13fbd8","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-cd067500-25b5-4eb4-835b-e31b56fdd3a3","text":"Ready for review.\n\n**Run.** All four modes at `87dd885`, clean tree, one binary. The `servaint-*` containers were stopped; they had been restarted between the two runs and were stopped again with the user's OK. Running containers were logged at every phase: only the baseline under test. Wall time: native-git 9.8 min, journal 9.9 min, MySQL 9 s, Dolt 13 s. MySQL 8.4.11 and Dolt 2.3.5 match `c1aa399`; Docker went 29.8.0 → 29.8.1. Dolt failed correctness again (lost updates, serialization errors; its 1k fixture stopped early). MySQL, journal and native-git passed everything.\n\n**Published:**\n- `latest.md`: new header (journal default, native-git audit mode), environment (Docker, Git-housekeeping row), summary and \"Changes since `c1aa399`\".\n- Results tables rendered from the JSON by a script validated to reproduce the `c1aa399` tables exactly.\n- The JSONs.\n- README: performance table generated from the JSON with the same formatter, plus date, revision and a default-mode sentence.\n\n**Noise control.** Two runs today (`f944eee`, `87dd885`) on near-identical code show single-client Git-heavy workloads varying up to ~25%, and MySQL p50s moved up to 1.5×. Only larger changes are claimed:\n- native-git write tails gone (rdb-fb3d12): Update x10 p95 1,415 → 182 ms, max 1,832 → 184;\n- native-git mixed throughput 22/26 → 98/318 ops/s (rdb-162912 + rdb-fb3d12);\n- native-git contended 4 → 7/6 ops/s, with rejections at the FIFO lock-step rate, explained;\n- publish + sync after writes 1,474 → 406 ms (native-git) and 1,270 → 837 ms (journal), and journal sync tails shrank (rdb-2124bc, rdb-fb3d12);\n- **regression, called out:** journal initial publish + sync 7.4 → 9.0 s. Bisected to rdb-fb3d12 with a bulk-checkpoint probe (5.2 → 6.1 s): smaller chunks (65 vs 78 rows/node) mean ~20% more objects. Follow-up filed as rdb-a0d510 (BACKLOG).\n\n**Investigations during this issue:**\n- the native-git contended regression bisected to rdb-e0c717, fixed in rdb-162912 before the final run;\n- the bulk-checkpoint slowdown bisected to rdb-fb3d12, giving rdb-a0d510.\n\n**Checks:** links resolve, `git diff --check` clean, `make lint`. rdb-c56142's last acceptance item (README and summary reflect the journal default) is met."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T06:14:55.009249Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
