{"id":"rdb-13fbd8","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DONE","sort_order":"as","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-02T06:26:35.880437Z","created_by":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-d690aa","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Journal durability levels (full / normal / off), default normal","description":"## Problem\n\nJournal-mode single-row writes take ~5 ms on macOS, against 0.7–1.1 ms for MySQL and 0.9–1.5 ms for Dolt in the scorecard. Measured breakdown of one autocommit `UPDATE` (50k-row table, mean of 300):\n\n| part | ms |\n|---|---:|\n| journal flush: `(*os.File).Sync` = `fcntl(F_FULLFSYNC)` on macOS | **4.82** |\n| journal append + encode + load | 0.09 |\n| SQL parse, plan, execute, transaction bookkeeping | 0.34 |\n\nFlush primitives on this machine (200-byte append, APFS internal SSD), p50:\n- `fsync()`: 0.035 ms;\n- `F_BARRIERFSYNC`: 0.23 ms;\n- `F_FULLFSYNC`: 5.0 ms.\n\nThe baselines don't pay this. MySQL in Docker Desktop is configured for full durability (`innodb_flush_log_at_trx_commit=1`, `sync_binlog=1`), but a synchronous write inside the VM costs 0.16 ms, so its flushes don't reach the Mac's SSD as full flushes. Natively on macOS, MySQL uses plain `fsync`, SQLite has `fullfsync` off by default, and PostgreSQL only uses `F_FULLFSYNC` with the opt-in `fsync_writethrough`. Go's `Sync()` is the reason RepoDB alone pays a full flush per commit.\n\n## Goal\n\nConfigurable journal durability, defaulting to **normal** (user decision, 2026-10-02):\n\n| level | macOS | Linux | Windows | an acknowledged commit survives |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| `full` | `F_FULLFSYNC` | `fdatasync` | `FlushFileBuffers` | power loss |\n| `normal` (default) | `F_BARRIERFSYNC` | `fdatasync` | `FlushFileBuffers` | process and OS crash; on power loss the most recent commits may be lost, but the journal never corrupts |\n| `off` | no flush per commit | same | same | process crash only |\n\nOn Linux and Windows, `normal` is a real flush, the same as `full`. The levels differ only where the OS offers a cheaper ordered flush.\n\nExpected on macOS with `normal`: single-row journal write ~5.2 → ~0.65 ms.\n\nSpec to follow. It covers the checkpoint full-flush rule, crash-safety reasoning, configuration surface, tests, docs, and dbbench reporting the level.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a8","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-d690aa","target_id":"rdb-8f75fc","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-d690aa","target_id":"rdb-13fbd8","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-d690aa","target_id":"rdb-19616b","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["feature","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T06:35:18.198065Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-d58a06","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"dbbench: run RepoDB inside Docker for apples-to-apples baseline comparison","description":"## Problem\n\nThe scorecard runs RepoDB natively on macOS (APFS, Go's `Sync()` = `F_FULLFSYNC`, ~5 ms) and MySQL and Dolt in Docker Desktop's Linux VM. There, a synchronous write costs ~0.16 ms because guest flushes don't reach the Mac's SSD as full flushes, and their data lives on Docker named volumes (ext4 inside the VM disk). Every write comparison mixes two storage stacks and two durability semantics.\n\n## Goal\n\nRun RepoDB's scorecard inside a Docker container on the same Docker Desktop VM, with its fixtures on a Docker named volume, so RepoDB, MySQL and Dolt share the kernel, filesystem, flush semantics and CPU limits.\n\nSpec to follow: image, volume, make target, report metadata, and how latest.md presents in-Docker vs host-native numbers.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a9","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-d58a06","target_id":"rdb-13fbd8","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["task","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T06:35:18.227248Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
