{"id":"rdb-48f066","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Speed up full scans and joins (binary Prolly nodes, projection pushdown, row counts)","description":"## Problem\n\nThe like-for-like scorecard (all systems in Docker, `636af3d`, latest.md) shows RepoDB's weakest spot is now whole-table reads. 50k rows, p50:\n\n| | MySQL 8.4 | Dolt 2.3 | RepoDB journal |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|\n| Full scan (`SELECT * FROM bench`) | 13 ms | 20 ms | 34 ms |\n| Join + aggregate (`SELECT a.name, COUNT(*) FROM bench b JOIN authors a ON a.id = 1 GROUP BY a.name`) | 2.6 ms | 12 ms | 45 ms |\n\nPoint reads, ranges, read transactions and writes are sub-millisecond or close to MySQL, so this is the main read gap.\n\n## Diagnosis (2026-10-02, 50k rows, journal, checkpointed, in-process on M1 Max)\n\n| Layer | Time |\n|---|---:|\n| JSON-decode every node of `bench` (767 nodes, 4.39 MB) | 17.8 ms, 108k allocs |\n| Same nodes length-prefixed binary, decoded without copying | 0.41 ms, 0 allocs |\n| Prolly iteration today | 20.0 ms |\n| SQL full scan / join / `COUNT(*)` | 31.8 / 35.5 / 27.6 ms |\n\n- About two thirds of a scan is the Prolly layer turning bytes into structures (JSON + base64 per node, plus a defensive copy of every cached node in `snapshotStore.Get`).\n- The rest is per row: `decodeRow`, re-encoding every row's key to check it against the stored key (`primaryRowIter`), `[]byte`→`string` key conversions in `mergedEntries`, go-mysql-server's iterator stack, and GC (29 MB garbage per scan).\n- **The join is not a planning problem.** `EXPLAIN` shows go-mysql-server already plans `a.id = 1` as an index lookup: a CrossJoin where one `authors` row drives one scan of `bench`. `bench` contributes no columns, yet every row is decoded. Past the scan, the cost is go-mysql-server's executor: hashing the group key through collation weight strings and building a joined row per row (~9 ms). MySQL's 2.6 ms comes from constant-table elimination plus an index-only count; matching that needs go-mysql-server changes, out of scope.\n- A decoded-node cache is unnecessary: binary nodes read in place cost almost nothing to \"decode\", and a cache would hold a second copy of every table.\n\n## Prototype (throwaway copy, cumulative)\n\n| Step | Full scan | Join | `COUNT(*)` | Point read |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| Today | 31.8 ms | 35.5 ms | 27.6 ms | 74 µs |\n| Binary nodes, no copy on cached `Get` | 14.3 | 17.7 | 9.6 | 32 µs |\n| + no per-row key re-encode, no key string conversion | 12.7 | 17.3 | 8.5 | |\n| + projection pushdown | ~12.7 | 12.8 | 4.3 | |\n\n## Directions\n\n1. **Binary Prolly node encoding (storage format 4 → 5, no migration).** Length-prefixed keys and values, raw 32-byte child hashes, subtree row counts in child links. `Store.Get` returns read-only bytes that may be shared; drop the defensive copies.\n2. **Iterate leaves in place:** the iterator walks a leaf's bytes instead of materializing an entry slice per node. Replaces the decoded-node cache idea.\n3. **Projection pushdown:** implement `sql.ProjectedTable` and decode only the requested columns; zero columns decodes nothing.\n4. **Per-row work:** drop the per-row key re-encode from scans (`ValidateSnapshot` already checks every stored row's key per snapshot); no key string conversions when there is no overlay.\n5. **Row counts:** implement `sql.StatisticsTable`; with no pending edits `RowCount` is exact from the root's link counts, so `SELECT COUNT(*) FROM t` becomes O(1).\n\nChunk-size tuning (rdb-a0d510) stays separate; it is another format bump, cheap during alpha.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- Full scan and join at 50k rows measured before and after with `make bench-docker` (journal), and the per-scan profile recorded in the issue.\n- Targets (in-process `go test` benchmarks, 50k rows): full scan ≤ 13 ms, join ≤ 15 ms, `SELECT COUNT(*)` ≤ 1 ms with no pending edits. In Docker: full scan at or below MySQL's 13 ms, join within 2× of Dolt's.","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-02T07:47:05.585216Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-a6a4d2","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Journal commits and reads cost O(pending edits): every commit re-writes the whole overlay","description":"## Problem\n\nFound while profiling rdb-48f066. In journal mode, the cost of every transaction grows with the number of uncheckpointed row edits (P), and the journal grows quadratically with the number of commits between checkpoints.\n\nMeasured 2026-10-02 (50k-row table, journal, M1 Max), with P rows inserted after the last checkpoint:\n\n| P | Single-row `UPDATE` | Journal bytes per `UPDATE` | Point read |\n|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 0 | 0.40 ms | 419 B | 87 µs |\n| 1,000 | 1.12 ms | 86 KB | 112 µs |\n| 10,000 | 6.79 ms | **856 KB** | 432 µs |\n\nThe scorecard never shows this because it checkpoints before its read and write workloads.\n\n## Causes\n\n1. **Each commit re-writes all pending edits.** `StartTransaction` copies the journal's decoded pending edits into `tableState.edits`, and the transaction's own edits go into the same map. `commitTypedEdits` then writes every entry of that map to the `typed-prepare` record. Replay merges edits key by key, so the inherited ones are redundant.\n2. **Each transaction copies the overlay.** `StartTransaction` copies the cached decoded overlay into a fresh map per table, even for a point read.\n3. **Each scan sorts the overlay.** `sortedOverlayKeys` filters and sorts all pending keys per scan interval.\n4. **Each new generation re-decodes everything.** After a commit, the next transaction misses the decoded-edits cache and decodes all P pending rows again, and `applyTypedEditsToSnapshot` copies every table's pending map twice per commit.\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- A commit appends only its own row edits: journal bytes per single-row `UPDATE` do not depend on P.\n- Starting a transaction and a point read do not copy or decode the overlay; a scan does not sort it.\n- Single-row `UPDATE` and point read at P = 10,000 within 1.5× of P = 0.\n- A regression test asserts the journal record size does not grow with P.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a9","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-a6a4d2","target_id":"rdb-48f066","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T07:47:05.616734Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
