{"id":"rdb-acd36d","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-34208848-3996-4dbd-b9af-246a8563fe83","text":"## Implemented (awaiting review)\n\n**What changed**\n- `common/prolly/reverse.go`: `EntryIterator` interface, and `Tree.ReverseIterator(ctx, end)`. Its stack of interior frames counts down, and each leaf is decoded whole and read backwards.\n- `repository.PendingRows.IterReverse(start, end)`: a k-way merge choosing the largest key, newest run winning.\n- Engine (`ranges.go`, `catalog.go`):\n  - `before(a, b, reverse)` orders keys for the scan direction;\n  - `sortedOverlayKeys`, `pendingCursor`, `rowOverlayCursor` and `mergedEntries` take a direction, and `mergedEntries` bounds by `start` when reversed;\n  - `scanOrder` visits intervals last to first;\n  - `rangePartition.reverse` and reverse point keys come from `lookup.IsReverse`;\n  - `primaryIndex` and `secondaryIndex` are `Reversible`.\n- Benchmark: `desc-limit20` added to `BenchmarkSQLAutocommitScans`.\n- Docs: `sql.md` (ordered scans both ways, NULLs last, PK-descending ties), `architecture.md`, `testing.md`, and the README roadmap item.\n\n**Measured** (in-process, 50k rows, journal): `ORDER BY id DESC LIMIT 20` takes 39 µs, against 41 µs ascending. It previously sorted the table: ~15 ms in-process, 18 ms in Docker.\n\n**Tests**\n- `TestReverseIteratorMatchesForward`: 0 to 5,000 entries, with bounds of none, before all keys, between keys, on a key and past the end.\n- `PendingRows` reverse checks in the model and interval tests.\n- The range model runs every predicate in both directions, plus DESC ordered limits including NULL index values, over native-git, journal with pending edits and open transactions.\n- The plan test asserts `reverse: true` and no Sort.\n- A mutation check (ignoring `IsReverse` for primary ranges) fails the model test as expected.\n\n`make test` and `make lint` pass."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:29:27.824962Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-a0d510","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"title":"Prolly chunking: mix the boundary hash and retune chunk sizes (format 6)","description":"Found in the rdb-13fbd8 scorecard refresh: journal \"initial publish + sync\" at 50k rows went 7.4 s (`c1aa399`) → 8.3–9.0 s. The 50k-row `bench` table had 767 tree nodes (65 rows/node) instead of 638 (78 rows/node), so a bulk publish writes and fsyncs ~20% more Git objects. The cause was traced to rdb-fb3d12's content-local boundary rule.\n\n## Root cause (measured 2026-10-02)\n\nThe boundary hash is FNV-64a of the key, tested on its **low bits**. FNV's last step is `(h ^ byte) * prime`, so its low 6 bits depend only on the low 6 bits of the last byte's xor. Sequential BIGINT keys (8-byte big-endian) differ in the last byte, so a boundary falls **exactly every 64 keys**, whatever the options say. The same effect is weaker for other keys.\n\nChunk simulation over 50k keys of each shape:\n\n| options, hash | BIGINT | string | random 16 B | index (grp, pk) |\n|---|---:|---:|---:|---:|\n| 32/128/6 bits, FNV (today) | 67.4 avg, 0% cap cuts | 80.3, 22% | 80.1, 20% | 72.9, 9% |\n| 32/128/6, FNV + splitmix64 | 81.7, 24% | 81.2, 23% | 80.4, 19% | 81.6, 22% |\n| 64/256/6, FNV (only options) | **67.5**, 0% | 123.8, 4% | 121.1, 4% | 107.8, 0% |\n| 64/256/6, FNV + splitmix64 | 125.3, 5% | 127.6, 3% | 120.2, 4% | 123.5, 4% |\n\nTuning the options alone can't fix BIGINT keys. A cut at the size cap depends on position rather than content, so the 20–24% cap cuts at today's options also hurt edit locality.\n\n## Direction\n\n- Mix FNV-64a with the splitmix64 finalizer before testing the low bits.\n- Set `DefaultOptions` to min 64, max 256, 6 bits: about 124 entries per leaf for every key shape, with about 4% cap cuts.\n- Storage format 5 → 6, no migration (alpha).\n\n## Acceptance\n\n- `bench` at 50k rows has about 400 leaves (was 742).\n- The edit-locality and history-independence tests pass.\n- Journal `initial_publish_sync` and native-git single-row update measured before and after. The trade-off: fewer objects to publish against larger leaves to rewrite per update.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a8","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:30:31.473245Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
