{"id":"rdb-a0d510","type":"UPDATE","payload":{"status":"DOING","sort_order":"a1","dependencies":null,"labels":null},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:30:35.870412Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-6bd369","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"PendingRows.With stable-sorts large commits slowly (bulk load +20% since rdb-a6a4d2)","description":"Found while measuring rdb-a0d510. A single-transaction bulk load of 50k rows (journal, the scorecard's `bulk_load`) takes about 166 ms on `main`, against about 138 ms at `062f8b3`. That's 3 × 5 runs each, in-process.\n\n**Cause:** the CPU profile puts 0.30 s of 3 s in `repository.PendingRows.With`, called from `applyTypedEditsToSnapshot` at commit. `With` orders the commit's edits with `sort.SliceStable` over 56-byte `TypedRowEdit` structs. Go's stable sort merges by rotation, O(n log² n) swaps, and a bulk commit brings 50k edits in one call.\n\n**Fix:** sort a permutation of indices with `slices.SortFunc`, breaking ties on the original position. That yields the same order as a stable sort with O(n log n) work and cheap swaps. Then build the deduplicated run in one pass.\n\n**Acceptance:** in-process bulk load back to about the `062f8b3` time, and the `PendingRows` tests unchanged.","status":"PLANNED","sort_order":"a8","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-6bd369","target_id":"rdb-a6a4d2","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-6bd369","target_id":"rdb-a0d510","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:48:35.723954Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-a0d510","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-58802f8b-ff46-4530-b2ed-e90b638c4999","text":"## Implemented (awaiting review)\n\n**What changed**\n- `common/prolly/tree.go`: `keyBoundaryHash` is FNV-64a plus the splitmix64 finalizer, used for leaf keys and link max keys. `DefaultOptions` are min 64, max 256, 6 bits.\n- `FormatVersion` 5 → 6; `snapshotValidationVersion` 2 → 3.\n- `chunking_internal_test.go`: Build 50k sequential BIGINT, string and random keys. Asserts average leaf sizes within 25% of each other and under 10% cap cuts. Result: 399 / 392 / 402 leaves.\n- Docs: `architecture.md` (chunk rule, format 6), `testing.md`.\n- Also fixes **rdb-6bd369** (filed during this work): `PendingRows.With` now sorts an index permutation instead of stable-sorting edits. In-process bulk load is back from 166 to 140 ms (`062f8b3`: 138 ms).\n\n**Measured, Docker, p50 at 50k rows, `main` → branch**\n\n| Workload | native-git | journal |\n|---|---:|---:|\n| initial_publish_sync | 173 → 166 ms | **793 → 568 ms** |\n| update_batch_1 | **18.7 → 14.9 ms** | 0.22 → 0.24 ms |\n| update_batch_100 | 22.4 → 17.6 ms | 2.74 → 3.01 ms |\n| insert / delete | 18.7 / 19.9 → 14.8 / 14.5 ms | noise (fsync jitter) |\n| full_scan | 17.6 → 12.9 ms | 13.4 → 13.2 ms |\n| bulk_load | 757 → 320 ms | 180 → 195 ms (before the rdb-6bd369 fix) |\n| divergent_edit_merge | 380 → 314 ms | 655 → 552 ms |\n| edit_sync_roundtrip | 167 → 131 ms | 251 → 226 ms |\n\nThe feared cost of larger leaves on native-git single-row writes did not appear. Writes got faster, because the shallower tree means fewer Git objects per commit.\n\nSingle-sample journal workloads (`publish_sync_after_writes`, `bulk_load`; n = 1) moved by up to 2× between two runs of the same code, so they aren't evidence either way. In-process `BenchmarkJournalCheckpoint` is flat (205/192/204 → 222/188/193 ms).\n\n`make test` and `make lint` pass."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:50:51.896325Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-6bd369","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-0a8f73da-0d67-4900-a6d4-13ecafec9397","text":"Fixed in the rdb-a0d510 branch, per the rule for bugs found during implementation. `With` sorts an `[]int32` permutation with `slices.SortFunc`, ties broken by position, then builds the deduplicated run in one pass.\n\nIn-process bulk load of 50k rows in one transaction (3 × 5 runs): `062f8b3` 136–143 ms; `main` 165–166 ms; fixed 139–142 ms. The `PendingRows` model tests pass unchanged. Closes with the rdb-a0d510 merge."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T09:50:51.927408Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
