{"id":"rdb-187370","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Concurrent sessions re-validate the whole database after another session commits","description":"Found by the rdb-db5459 workload profiling (journal mode, `mutable` group, 100k issues, Docker).\n\n**Symptom.** `issue_mixed_concurrent` with 16 clients drops from 1,543 ops/s at 1k issues to **14 ops/s (p95 1.6 s)** at 100k. MySQL does 1,431 ops/s. `append_concurrent` with 16 clients drops from 784 to 167 ops/s. With one client, the same workloads stay fast (sub-ms p50).\n\n**Cause.** When a session starts a transaction after any other session has committed, `session.resolveSnapshot` (`engine/catalog.go`) sees a new (commit, generation) and calls `ValidateSnapshot` (`engine/engine.go`). The validation cache is keyed by generation, so every journal commit misses it. A miss then:\n- calls `loadTable` → `tableState.ensureRows` for **every table**, which decodes and clones every row;\n- runs `prolly.Reachable` over every data and index tree.\n\nSo each transaction start after a concurrent commit costs O(database). One client never pays it, because its own commit already updates `db.snapshot`.\n\n**Evidence** (CPU profile, `dbbench -cpuprofile`, `-tagfocus issue_mixed_concurrent`): 85.2 s of 87.9 s (97%) is under `StartTransaction → resolveSnapshot → ValidateSnapshot`. Of that, 73 s is in `loadTable/ensureRows` (row decode, `prolly.clone`) and 12 s in `prolly.Reachable`. A heap profile taken just before the 16-client phase shows 745 MB of the 1.05 GB heap held by `prolly.clone` from that path. That accounts for most of the journal run's 6.2 GB peak RSS.\n\n**Direction (to spec).** A journal generation built from a validated base plus the engine's own typed edits doesn't need full re-validation. Validate incrementally: only the trees or chunks that changed since the last validated snapshot, or carry validation forward on commit. Don't materialise rows to validate.\n\n**Acceptance.** `issue_mixed_concurrent` with 16 clients at 100k issues should come within ~2× of its 1k throughput, and the heap should no longer scale with table size during concurrent work.","sort_order":"ae","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-187370","target_id":"rdb-db5459","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-187370","target_id":"rdb-df092b","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:11:53.312229Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-93103f","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Snapshot load reads and SHA-256 checks every object; sync loads several snapshots (O(database))","description":"Found by the rdb-db5459 workload profiling (journal mode, Docker).\n\n**Symptom.** For the same write burst (600 edits per round), the time to sync grows with database size, not with the size of the change:\n\n| issues | sync per round | fixture growth per round |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1k | 230 ms | 0.7 MB |\n| 10k | 1.1 s | 4.2 MB |\n| 100k | 5.7 s | 11 MB |\n\nThe append group shows the same pattern for 1,000 new events: 300 ms at 1k, 1.2 s at 100k. Peer pull after growth takes 6.2 s at 100k issues. The initial publish of 100k issues takes 16 s, against 7.5 s in native-git.\n\n**Cause.** `Repository.loadSnapshot` (`common/repository/repository.go`) is eager. It lists the whole tree (`ListTreeObjects`), reads **every** object through `git.CLI.ReadObjects`, and SHA-256 checks each one into an in-memory cache. Sync then loads full snapshots several times: `SnapshotCommit`, `OpenWithSnapshot`, and `requireCleanWorkingState → WorkingState.load → snapshotCommit`.\n\n**Evidence.**\n- A heap profile during the 100k growth phase shows 715 MB of 775 MB in `loadSnapshot`: `ReadObjects` 331 MB plus `bytes.growSlice` 384 MB, split evenly between `OpenWithSnapshot` and `SnapshotCommit`.\n- In-process CPU in sync is only about 1.3 s per round, including `storage.Sum` at 1.4 s over 3 rounds. Most of the 5.7 s is inside the Git subprocesses reading every object.\n\n**Direction (to spec).** Read and verify objects lazily, on first `Get`, or verify only objects new since a trusted, already-validated commit. Avoid repeated full loads of the same commit within one sync. rdb-2c93ca already covers the second part, for subprocess count; this issue is about the O(database) cost per load.","sort_order":"af","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-93103f","target_id":"rdb-db5459","kind":"relates_to"},{"source_id":"rdb-93103f","target_id":"rdb-2c93ca","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:11:53.348031Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-db5459","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"generic","filename":"reference-growth-series.csv","action":"created"},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:14:59.732094Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-db5459","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-2fd14318-9377-43a5-a794-d2600cd48081","text":"## Findings: append-heavy and mutable workloads (suite v2 reference runs)\n\n**Setup.** `make bench-docker BENCH_ALLOW_DIRTY=1 BENCH_ARGS='-workloads append,mutable'` with the defaults: 1k/10k/100k rows, 1/4/16 clients, 30 requests, 10 growth rounds. All four modes ran one after another on the same Docker VM (M1 Max host, VM with 4 CPUs and 16 GB), on AC power: journal (durability `normal`), native-git, MySQL 8.4.11 and Dolt 2.3.5. Source is `6eceedd` plus this issue's harness change only, so the reports record a dirty tree. **Every check passed in all four modes.** These results are not published; `latest.md` waits for the next scorecard refresh (decision 3). The growth series is attached as `reference-growth-series.csv`, with all rounds for RepoDB and the first and last rounds at 100k for the baselines.\n\n### At 100k rows (p50 / p95 ms; ops/s)\n\n| workload | journal | native-git | MySQL | Dolt |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| events_recent | 0.36 / 0.46 | 0.38 / 0.50 | 0.27 / 0.44 | 0.54 / 0.88 |\n| events_session | 0.31 / 0.49 | 0.31 / 0.48 | 0.25 / 0.34 | 0.54 / 0.84 |\n| events_kind_count (10k-row tail) | 25.4 / 28.6 | 25.3 / 27.4 | **7.9 / 8.1** | 20.1 / 21.3 |\n| append_single | 0.51 / 0.64 | 46.7 / 51.3 | 1.17 / 1.99 | 0.91 / 1.51 |\n| append_batch_100 | 2.9 / 3.9 | 51.4 / 55.3 | 11.6 / 17.3 | 38.9 / 44.1 |\n| append_concurrent, 16 clients | **167 ops/s**, 41 conflicts | 13 ops/s, 431 conflicts | 6,145 ops/s | 982 ops/s |\n| issue_point_read | 0.06 / 0.09 | 0.06 / 0.09 | 0.07 / 0.20 | 0.30 / 0.44 |\n| issue_board_query | 0.20 / 0.26 | 0.19 / 0.27 | 0.40 / 4.43 | 32.7 / 47.1 |\n| issue_assignee_query (~3.6k rows) | 25.8 / 29.5 | 29.1 / 32.8 | 67.1 / 87.6 | 7.2 / 7.9 |\n| issue_update_status | 0.75 / 1.19 | 101 / 113 | 0.82 / 1.17 | 1.04 / 1.32 |\n| issue_comment (tx) | 0.80 / 0.94 | 93 / 96 | 1.03 / 1.45 | 1.60 / 1.98 |\n| issue_mixed_concurrent, 1 client | 330 ops/s | 32 | 127 | 125 |\n| issue_mixed_concurrent, 16 clients | **14 ops/s, p95 1,606** | 139 ops/s, p95 79 | 1,431 ops/s, p95 94 | 384 ops/s, p95 164 |\n| peer pull after growth (mutable) | 6.2 s | 14.8 s | – | – |\n| process peak RSS | 6.2 GB | 3.3 GB | 50 MB* | 43 MB* |\n\n\\*The harness only; server memory is not measured.\n\n**Scaling of the same work (journal).**\n- `issue_mixed_concurrent` with 16 clients: 1,543 → 338 → 14 ops/s across 1k / 10k / 100k.\n- Sync per growth round (600 edits): 230 ms → 1.15 s → 5.7 s.\n- Sync per append round (1,000 events): 300 ms → 400 ms → 1.2 s.\n- Fixture growth per mutable round: 0.7 → 4.4 → 11 MB, which is about 19 KB of Git objects per edit at 100k.\n- Heap at the end of a mutable round: 30 MB → 139 MB → 1.1 GB.\n\n### Ranked bottlenecks\n\n1. **Concurrent sessions re-validate the whole database. Filed as rdb-187370.** This hits both shapes, in the default persistence mode, and it's the largest gap against MySQL (100× at 16 clients).\n   - Mechanism: after any other session commits, `resolveSnapshot` sees a new generation, and `ValidateSnapshot` decodes and clones every row of every table (`loadTable/ensureRows`) and walks every tree (`Reachable`).\n   - CPU profile (Linux container, `-tagfocus issue_mixed_concurrent`): **97%** of the workload's CPU is under `StartTransaction → resolveSnapshot → ValidateSnapshot`. That's 73 s in row decode and clone and 12 s in `Reachable`, out of 87.9 s.\n   - Heap profile before the 16-client phase: **745 MB of the 1.05 GB** heap is `prolly.clone` from that path. This also explains most of the 6.2 GB peak RSS.\n2. **Sync and snapshot loading are O(database). Filed as rdb-93103f, related to rdb-2c93ca.**\n   - Mechanism: `loadSnapshot` reads every object through `cat-file` and SHA-256 checks each one, and a sync loads full snapshots several times.\n   - Heap during growth sync: 715 MB of 775 MB in `loadSnapshot` (`ReadObjects` plus buffer growth), split between `OpenWithSnapshot` and `SnapshotCommit`.\n   - In-process sync CPU is only about 1.3 s of each 5.7 s round; the rest is spent in Git subprocesses.\n   - This sets the cost of every sync, peer pull and initial publish (16 s for 100k issues).\n3. **Disjoint-key writes conflict. This is existing work: rdb-40ba7c, under epic rdb-df092b.**\n   - In journal mode, `append_concurrent` with 16 clients rejects 204 of 480 attempts at 1k, even though every insert uses a fresh key. MySQL rejects none.\n   - It matters less than item 1 at 100k (41 rejections), but it caps write scaling for the append shape.\n4. **Storage write amplification. This informs retention and compaction work: rdb-515fae and rdb-93382e.**\n   - Journal checkpoints write about 19 KB of new Git objects per hot-row edit at 100k (11 MB per 600 edits).\n   - Native-git writes about **330 KB of loose objects per autocommit** at 100k, reaching 2 GB after 6,000 edits, which makes the peer pull 14.8 s.\n   - A separate 10k native-git run (about 1,820 commits) measured the local repository at 280 MB loose and 67 MB after `git gc`. The remote, which is fully packed, holds about 34 KB per commit. So packing removes most of the size, but the per-edit cost stays well above the size of the change.\n5. **Native-git write latency grows with table size.** `issue_update_status` takes 12 ms at 1k, 27 ms at 10k and 101 ms at 100k; appends take 16 → 22 → 47 ms. The native-git heap also climbs across growth rounds, from 1.0 to 2.0 GB at 100k, where journal stays flat. *Not profiled.* My hypothesis is that each autocommit pays item 2's snapshot load, plus a cache that holds per-commit snapshots. That needs a profile before anyone files it. Journal is now the default (rdb-c56142), so this ranks below items 1–4.\n6. **`events_kind_count` is 3× slower than MySQL.** It's a `GROUP BY` over a 10k-row `ts` range through the secondary index: 25 ms against 7.9 ms. That's about 2.5 µs per row, consistent with a primary-row lookup and decode for each index entry. This one is modest, and it's a candidate for covering-index or projection work.\n\n### What doesn't need work\n\n- Point reads, the recent-events window, session scans and the board query match MySQL or beat it at every size.\n- The assignee query is 2.6× faster than MySQL, though Dolt is faster still.\n- Single-client journal writes take 0.5–0.9 ms at 100k, faster than MySQL and Dolt.\n- Write latency is flat across growth rounds in journal mode, so history depth doesn't slow writes there.\n\n**Suggested order before the epics:** rdb-187370, then rdb-93103f (with rdb-2c93ca folded in or done right after), then rdb-40ba7c. Re-run `-workloads append,mutable` after each one to confirm the change."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:15:37.277458Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-db5459","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-b035f89e-9020-4edb-a86c-780b528e095f","text":"## Completion: harness changes\n\n- **`experiments/dbbench/workloads.go` (new).** The `append` and `mutable` groups, written once against a `conn`/`backend` interface, with RepoDB (`repoBackend`) and external (`externalBackend`) implementations. It also holds the deterministic fixtures (pseudo-prose filler), the Zipf hot-row picker (skewed toward the newest issues), the growth phase with its `series` points, the peer clone with checksum comparison, and external deadlock-to-conflict mapping (MySQL errors 1213 and 1205).\n- **`main.go` / `external.go`.**\n  - Suite v2.\n  - New flags: `-workloads`, `-workload-rows` (default 1k/10k/100k, minimum 1k) and `-growth-rounds` (default 10).\n  - Reports gain `config.workloads`, `config.workload_rows`, `config.growth_rounds`, a per-measurement `group`, and a `series` array.\n  - The printed table has a section per group plus a growth summary.\n  - Diagnostic flags `-cpuprofile` (samples labelled by workload) and `-pprof`.\n  - The core group is unchanged.\n- **Tests (`workloads_test.go`).** `-workloads` parsing, fixture determinism and shape, the hot-picker's bounds, skew and seed stability, growth series with and without sync, peer-divergence reporting, and conflict mapping. `go test ./...` passes; gofmt and vet (including the Windows target) are clean.\n- **Docs.** `docs/benchmark.md` documents workload contract v2 (core, then append and mutable, the growth phase, the new flags, profiling, external mode and coverage limits). `docs/testing.md` lists the new tests.\n- **Verification.** Docker smoke runs (1k, 3 rounds) and full reference runs passed in journal, native-git, MySQL and Dolt. The findings are in the comment above.\n- **Follow-ups filed.** rdb-187370 and rdb-93103f (both BACKLOG, linked).\n\n**Decisions not in the original spec** (all recorded in the spec's implementation notes):\n- Setup steps are prefixed with the group name; workload names are not.\n- `event_update_rare` is a standalone workload, and the 1-in-100 interleaving happens in the growth bursts.\n- Hot rows are skewed toward the newest issues. The measured skew is stronger than 80/20.\n- The reference runs used a dirty tree.\n- The profiling flags were added after the reference runs; they don't change any workload."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:15:37.305962Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
