{"id":"rdb-4d8ccb","type":"CREATE","payload":{"title":"Secondary-index scans do one full Prolly point lookup per matching row","description":"Found while verifying rdb-187370 (journal mode, dbbench `mutable` group, 100k issues, Docker).\n\n**Symptom.** `issue_assignee_query` (`SELECT id, title, status FROM issues WHERE assignee = ? AND status \u003c\u003e 'done'`, served by the index `by_assignee (assignee, status)`) runs at 30 ops/s with a 32.75 ms p50 at 100k issues. With 20 assignees each query returns a few thousand rows. That query is 10% of `issue_mixed_concurrent`, and it alone caps the mix at about 300 ops/s with one client at 100k (1,466 ops/s at 1k). After rdb-187370, 16-client throughput is 963 ops/s at 100k vs 4,311 at 1k. That gap comes from this query, not from concurrency.\n\n**Evidence** (Linux CPU profile, `-tagfocus workload=issue_mixed_concurrent`, after rdb-187370): 1.67 s of the workload's 1.95 s is in the assignee query. Of that, 1.62 s is `indexRowIter.Next → tableState.lookupRow → prolly.(*Tree).Get`. Each matching index entry does an independent root-to-leaf descent of the primary tree (node open, varint decode, `prolly.clone`).\n\n**Direction (to spec).** Amortise the PK lookups of an index scan. For example, sort or batch the PKs from the index range and walk the primary tree with one cursor (`IteratorFrom` / seek forward), or keep the last leaf path between lookups. Also check whether `clone` is needed on this read path.","status":"BACKLOG","sort_order":"ae","dependencies":[{"source_id":"rdb-4d8ccb","target_id":"rdb-187370","kind":"relates_to"}],"labels":["bug","performance"]},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:48:31.695833Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-187370","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-9f519385-5706-47e6-b64d-e663ba0bedef","text":"**Implementation complete — awaiting review.**\n\n**Changes**\n- `engine/engine.go`: the snapshot-keyed validation LRU is replaced by a **per-table** LRU (1024 entries). The key is repository identity, base commit, version, `SchemaRoot`, `DataRoot` and the sorted index roots. `ValidateSnapshot` validates only tables that miss. `validatedTableObjects` keeps its signature and reads the same cache. `snapshotValidationVersion` → 4.\n- New `validateTable`/`validateTableRows`: rows are checked (decode, then the re-encoded PK must equal the stored key) by streaming `tree.Iterator`. No row map is built. `loadTable` had no callers left and was removed.\n- `engine/performance.go`: new `TablesValidated` counter.\n- `docs/architecture.md`: the SQL-validation paragraph describes the per-table cache and streaming.\n- Test `TestOtherEngineCommitSkipsValidationOfUnchangedTables` (`engine/cross_engine_test.go`): after another engine's journal commits, the reader validates 0 tables. After DDL on one table, it validates exactly 1. With the old generation-keyed cache the same test reports 10.\n\n**Not in the spec:** tables with identical roots now share a cache entry (content-keyed), which is correct. The cache key has no table name.\n\n**Verification**\n- `GOOS=windows go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./...`: pass. `go test -race ./common/repository ./engine ./integration`: pass.\n- Docker, journal, `-workloads mutable` (dirty-tree diagnostic run):\n\n| issue_mixed_concurrent | 1 client | 4 clients | 16 clients |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| 1k | 1,466 | 2,242 | 4,311 |\n| 10k | 1,802 | 2,673 | 3,419 |\n| 100k (before) | — | — | **14** (p95 1.6 s) |\n| 100k (after) | 255 | 888 | **911** (p95 48 ms) |\n\n  Peak RSS 2.05 GB (was 6.2 GB). A second profiled run gave 963 ops/s at 16 clients.\n- Linux CPU profile of `issue_mixed_concurrent` at 100k: 1.95 s total (was 87.9 s). `StartTransaction` is 0.02 s, and `ValidateSnapshot`/`ensureRows` no longer appear.\n\n**Acceptance \"within ~2× of 1k throughput\": not met (4.7×).** The remaining gap is not concurrency: the 1-client mix shows the same gap (1,466 → 255). 86% of the remaining CPU is the assignee query, with one Prolly `Tree.Get` per matching index row. Filed as **rdb-4d8ccb**.\n\n**Heap.** Validation no longer shows up in heap profiles. The remaining 100k heap during the run is in `Checkpoint`:\n- `SnapshotCommit` reads all objects (~330 MB), which is rdb-93103f.\n- Journal compaction decodes the whole journal (~630 MB at the bulk-load checkpoint, in `readJournalFrames`). No issue filed for this."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T11:48:43.194393Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
