{"id":"rdb-4d8ccb","type":"ARTIFACT","payload":{"artifact_type":"spec","filename":"spec.md","action":"updated"},"created_at":"2026-10-02T12:08:45.495848Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
{"id":"rdb-4d8ccb","type":"COMMENT","payload":{"id":"cmt-adf211cb-3fd6-469e-81b9-99b0436955ac","text":"**Implementation complete — awaiting review.**\n\n**Approach changed during implementation (spec updated, approved).** The path-reusing `prolly.Lookup` cursor from the first spec was prototyped and reverted: 48 ms/op vs 34.5 ms before, with 3× the allocations. A Linux profile showed that 1.65 s of `Tree.Get`'s 2.0 s was the *leaf* linear scan (about 160 entries of about 600 bytes per leaf). A cursor can't skip that.\n\n**Changes**\n- `common/prolly/offsets.go` (new): a process-wide, bounded cache of each node's item start offsets, keyed by node hash (16 shards × 4096 nodes, arbitrary eviction). On a miss it parses the node once and requires `finish()`. `nodeReader.search(key)` binary-searches the offsets, parsing only the probed keys.\n- `common/prolly/tree.go`: `Tree.Get` uses `search` at every level. Signature and behaviour unchanged. A node is now fully framing-checked on first `Get`.\n- `common/prolly/get_test.go`: `Get` agrees with the iterator on trees of 1, 2, 255 and 20k entries. Missing keys before, between and after the entries return `ErrNotFound`. Nodes with trailing bytes or truncation fail with a decode error.\n- `engine/scan_bench_test.go`: `BenchmarkSQLAutocommitIndexScan`, a 100k-row journal table shaped like `issues` running the assignee query.\n- `docs/architecture.md`: describes the point-read path.\n\n**Verification**\n- `GOOS=windows go build ./...`, `go vet ./...`, `go test ./...`: pass. `go test -race ./common/prolly ./engine ./integration`: pass.\n- `BenchmarkSQLAutocommitIndexScan` (macOS): **34.5 → 9.6 ms/op** (3.6×). Allocations are unchanged (7.1 MB, 61k).\n- Other engine benchmarks (`SQLAutocommitScans`, `SQLAutocommitPointRead`, `SQLWarmReads`, before and after on the same host): all flat. A first pass showed 1k point reads about 4–12% slower. Alternating re-runs showed that was noise: after is 25.2–26.7 µs vs before 24.8–29.3 µs.\n- Docker, journal, `-workloads mutable` (dirty-tree diagnostic run; compared with the rdb-187370 run):\n\n| 100k | before | after |\n|---|---|---|\n| issue_assignee_query | 30.5 ops/s, p50 32.75 ms | **66.4 ops/s, p50 14.65 ms** (2.2×) |\n| issue_mixed_concurrent ×1 | 255.5 | **517.9** |\n| issue_mixed_concurrent ×4 | 888.2 | 1,059.3 |\n| issue_mixed_concurrent ×16 | 910.8 | 1,202.1 |\n\n  The 1k and 10k rows of this run moved in both directions by up to about 30%. Those are 30-request samples, and the race tests were running at the same time. The Go benchmarks above show no regression.\n\n**Not in the spec:** none."},"created_at":"2026-10-02T12:21:54.101444Z","created_by":"claude-code/2.1.286 \u003cagent@mcp\u003e","on_behalf_of":"Nicolas Bettenburg \u003cnicbet@gmail.com\u003e","source":"mcp"}
